\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The \uc2 EEOC\uc2 's Unjust  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Mandate; Catholic bishops are suing over an attempt to rewrite a law protecting pregnant women at work.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Timothy P. Broglio \par \uc2 537 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2024\par 21:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops cheered when Congress passed the Pregnant Workers Fairness Act in 2022. The bill's purpose was laudable and obvious: to expand protections for pregnant women in the workplace. Yet the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  has subverted the law's noble goal by turning it into an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -accommodation mandate. That is unjust and illegal. In federal court last week, the USCCB filed suit to challenge it.\par \par \uc2 The bill is pro-woman, pro-family and pro-worker. It is also bipartisan. The text requires employers to offer reasonable accommodations\u8212\'20\'14such as paid time off or modified work schedules\u8212\'20\'14for the various needs that arise during pregnancy and childbirth. It bars employers from denying employment opportunities to a pregnant employee because of these needs, or from taking adverse action against her.\par \par \uc2 Our commitment to promoting human dignity must include defending the unborn and supporting women in their pregnancies. That includes advocating dignified conditions in the workplace. The PWFA advances each of these principles.\par \par \uc2 Yet the \uc2 EEOC\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14the agency charged with implementing the statute\u8212\'20\'14has twisted it to undermine human dignity. Under the commission's "final rule" for the law, set to take effect June 18, employers must facilitate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the \uc2 EEOC\uc2 's telling, having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the equivalent of pregnancy or childbirth. Required employer accommodations would include offering paid leave to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and employers who express pro-life perspectives could be subject to legal liability.\par \par \uc2 The commission distorts the law beyond recognition. The PWFA never mentions \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Its congressional sponsors, from Sen. Bob Casey (D., Pa.) to Sen. \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  (R., La.), explicitly said it couldn't be read to include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They emphasized that the provisions are simple and that it is difficult to imagine a less controversial bill. They were right and the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  is wrong. The bill wouldn't have passed, and we wouldn't have supported it, if it meant what the commission says.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 EEOC\uc2  knows that. When it first proposed the rule in August 2023, it received comments from more than 54,000 individuals and organizations opposing it. The commission persisted, betraying the public's trust and transforming bipartisan legislation into a radical regulation.\par \par \uc2 In addition to shoehorning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a life-affirming statute, the regulation effectively nullifies the new law's protections for religious employers. It also bans employers from encouraging employees to choose life instead of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That means religious employers cannot require their employees to be faithful advocates of the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 The commission is turning the plain text of the law on its head to promote \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the \uc2 EEOC\uc2 's rule is allowed to stand, the USCCB and Catholic ministries nationwide will be penalized for doing what they have done for centuries: teaching and serving the infinite dignity of every person. We will not compromise that twofold mission of walking with women and honoring the unborn. We look forward to the courts' vindicating our right to do so.\par \par \uc2 Archbishop Broglio is president of the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240528ek5s004v1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
