\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 What Florida Can Learn From Georgia's Six-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban; Neighboring state's experience offers clues: busy clinics, short time frames and constant uncertainty\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto | Photographs by Alyssa Pointer for The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 992 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2024\par 09:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ATLANTA\u8212\'20\'14When Georgia banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  around six weeks of pregnancy in 2022, the number of patients seeking the procedure at the Feminist Women's Health Center dwindled to a trickle, confirming worries from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates who predicted the law would operate much like a total ban. Then, after the initial shock, the appointment traffic came back.\par \par \uc2 On a recent warm April day, the suburban Atlanta facility was almost as busy as it ever was. It has been providing abortions three days a week, seeing about 30 patients a day. And the clinic recently added a fourth day to accommodate demand.\par \par \uc2 "A six-week ban is not a total ban," said Kwajelyn Jackson, the clinic's executive director. While operating under the new limits, the facility is "ultimately able to see thousands of patients," she said.\par \par \uc2 In neighboring Florida, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/biden-pushes-abortion-message-in-trumps-home-state-of-florida-024370bb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six-week ban takes effect }}}\uc2  Wednesday, creating additional regionwide anxieties in the South, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has narrowed dramatically {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-dobbs-year-after-roe-support-politics-d1ef5a5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  two years ago. If Georgia is any guide, Florida can expect a complicated landscape ahead: Fewer overall abortions but continued access for women who act fast, and bustling activity at clinics, though uncertainty for doctors and patients.\par \par \uc2 While more than a dozen states have bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout pregnancy, restrictions at or around six weeks are less common and more poorly understood. These so-called heartbeat laws generally prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  once fetal cardiac activity can be detected. For Georgia, the limits have reduced abortions by about half, compared with activity before the ban.\par \par \uc2 Before the Supreme Court withdrew the federal constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , women had a right to choose the procedure until fetal viability, or around 23 weeks of pregnancy. While very few abortions happened that late, the six-week deadline comes soon after many pregnant women miss their first period.\par \par \uc2 Florida's Republican-led legislature {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/florida-abortion-ban-law-28c65d8c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed the six-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban a year ago }}}\uc2 , but it wasn't cleared to take effect until a recent state Supreme Court ruling. That court also allowed a referendum on the November ballot that, if approved, would restore broad protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 South Carolina's Supreme Court allowed a heartbeat law in recent months, and lawmakers in Iowa are asking the high court there to allow a six-week ban to take effect.\par \par \uc2 Georgia's six-week window has prompted women to pay closer attention to when they might be pregnant and visit clinics earlier, researchers and providers say. Women are calling the Feminist Women's Health Center if their period is even one day late or they have the first hint of early pregnancy symptoms like nausea, clinic staffers said. An increase in the number of patients coming from states with total bans also has helped keep numbers at clinics up.\par \par \uc2 Most patients at the clinic take medication to end their pregnancies, though the center still does perform a number of surgical procedures.\par \par \uc2 The ban offers a tight and unpredictable window of just a couple of weeks for most women to get an appointment after being able to learn they are pregnant. Some patients take time off work, find child care and procure funds for the procedure only to be turned away after an ultrasound reveals it is too late. If patients take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and they don't work\u8212\'20\'14which occasionally happens\u8212\'20\'14it is sometimes too late to try again, Georgia providers say.\par \par \uc2 "We can't provide care and that can be really hard," said Lisa Haddad, an Atlanta obstetrician-gynecologist.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What impact do you think a six-week ban in Florida will have on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue nationally? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Jackson said her clinic's financial situation has become more challenging. It used to perform abortions up to 22 weeks of pregnancy and was able to charge more for later abortions. That helped offset the money it loses on procedures early in pregnancy that require similar levels of staffing but cost less to the patient. One of the two rooms the clinic formerly used for surgical procedures is piled high with cardboard boxes and the equipment is covered in sheets.\par \par \uc2 Many Georgia providers seem to be hanging on, despite the challenges. Overall, the number of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics actually increased slightly in the couple of years since the ban took effect, according to a database run by the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In some circumstances, the Georgia law has pushed women to continue pregnancies they might otherwise have ended. Neesha Lewis found out she was pregnant shortly after the six-week law took effect. She knew nothing about the new restrictions and learned it was too late for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointment.\par \par \uc2 Lewis, 33 years old and raising four children as a single mother, said friends and family pushed her to travel to a clinic out of state. She has had abortions previously but said the law put up a roadblock that helped her decide to keep the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 For months she wrestled with whether she would keep the child herself and went to an adoption agency, where she wrote down the life she envisioned for the son she would soon deliver: both a mother and a father, maybe even a backyard and a dog. When she found a Florida family who checked all those boxes, she made up her mind on adoption.\par \par \uc2 Lewis had to undergo an emergency caesarean under general anesthesia. When she woke up after the ordeal, the baby's new father was cradling him and saying how proud he was of her son. "I'm so thankful I did not go with Option A," she said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240501ek51001e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
