\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 The Tragic Pregnancy Stories Filling the  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Campaign Airwaves; Biden, once reluctant to talk about  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 , is sharing the microphone with women in battleground states\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Lucey \par \uc2 1,575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2024\par 14:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amanda Zurawski has only opened a box of items she lovingly collected during her pregnancy a few times. The baby book, the blanket, stuffed animals and the tiny clothes reminded her of the harrowing days when she learned her pregnancy wasn't viable.\par \par \uc2 But last month she volunteered to go through the box, on camera, for a campaign ad on behalf of President Biden. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas law restricting \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  initially prevented Zurawski, at 18 weeks pregnant, from getting an induction \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The 37-year-old contracted a serious infection before eventually delivering a stillborn baby girl.\par \par \uc2 Zurawski is one of a group of women the Biden campaign has tapped as surrogates to help convince voters they should back Democrats this fall to protect {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access }}}\uc2 . The approach is designed to undermine presumptive Republican presidential nominee \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who as president appointed three justices to the Supreme Court that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned the 1973 Roe v. Wade decision }}}\uc2  in June 2022.\par \par \uc2 Zurawski started sharing her story weeks after her experience and became the lead plaintiff in a lawsuit against the state of Texas. Along the way, she attracted the attention of the White House.\par \par \uc2 "I get asked all the time by reporters: 'Do you have any of the baby's things?' And most of the time I say, 'Yes, but no, you can't see it, because it's excruciating for me to go through that,' " Zurawski said during an interview in Milwaukee. When she brainstormed with the campaign about the ad, she told them "For this, I would be happy to show some of her things if you think that it would be worthwhile."\par \par \uc2 The resulting ad is titled "Willow's Box," named for the baby girl she delivered stillborn. It shows a tearful Zurawski slowly holding up the items before collapsing into her husband in tears. At the end, across a black screen appear the words: "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did this."\par \par \uc2 Women telling stories like these have introduced the president and vice president at rallies and appeared at Democratic events. In recent weeks, Zurawski campaigned in Wisconsin and North Carolina with Kaitlyn Joshua, 31, who said she was denied a surgical procedure for a miscarriage in Baton Rouge, La. Joshua also appeared with Biden at a Florida rally this past week.\par \par \uc2 Biden, an 81-year-old Catholic, has at times been a reluctant messenger on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14though he has appeared in television ads talking about his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and campaigned on it recently in Florida, where he stood below signs that said "Restore Roe." His campaign has specifically tapped surrogates who tell stories of wanted pregnancies but also ran into obstacles getting medical care due to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. It hasn't highlighted women who sought elective abortions.\par \par \uc2 Locked in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-biden-poll-swing-states-ad594acb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a close race }}}\uc2  with Trump, Biden's campaign hopes that the support of these women will help forge a connection with key voters Biden needs to shore up, namely {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/abortion-poll-trump-swing-state-voters-2024-election-3e12f57c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 women in the politically powerful suburbs }}}\uc2 . A recent Wall Street Journal poll of seven battleground states showed that 39% of suburban women cited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a make-or-break issue. That poll also showed that voters overall saw Biden as better able to handle \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than Trump.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has known Zurawski for over a year and was the first person from the White House or the campaign to meet with many of these women.\par \par \uc2 "The impact of it is to hopefully remind people this is not just some academic or intellectual or political issue," Harris said in an interview. "This is having a real effect on real people on a daily basis that is resulting in suffering."\par \par \uc2 Harris makes an effort to stay in touch with the women, saying she wants to remind them "that they're not alone, and that what they are experiencing matters and that there are people who are gonna stand up and fight for them." Zurawski said Harris called her recently to check in and "acknowledged how difficult it must be."\par \par \uc2 It is an approach that has proven effective in past elections. In 2022, Kentucky Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  won re-election in a conservative state with a campaign that focused heavily on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and personal testimony. The Democrat ran an ad that went viral featuring a woman who was raped as a 12-year-old and asserted that his opponent would force someone in that situation to have the baby.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have struggled with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the high court ruling, which unfurled a patchwork of laws across the country. Trump has boasted about appointing three of the justices who overturned Roe, but he also has sought to distance himself from a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, saying the issue {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/abortion-donald-trump-2024-election-fe641cc5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 should be left with the states }}}\uc2 . The risks associated with that approach became clear when Arizona's highest court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-ban-821501cb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revived a Civil War-era ban }}}\uc2  on most abortions, putting pressure on Trump to weigh in.\par \par \uc2 Trump campaign spokeswoman Karoline Leavitt said Trump has long supported letting states make their own calls on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion groups say they have their own efforts to promote the stories of women who regret abortions or are glad they didn't have one, an approach that has historically been part of their messaging.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of a ballot initiative last year in Ohio on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America aired a television ad featuring a woman who said she survived an attempt by her mother to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Students for Life is planning a campaign in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures on the ballot called "Almost Aborted," with personal testimonies from mothers who say they were advised to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but didn't. And Focus on the Family has promoted the stories of women who had adverse diagnoses and chose not to terminate their pregnancies, including a woman who was told her baby had a fatal condition and chose not to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , giving birth to a daughter who lived only briefly.\par \par \uc2 "The GOP needs to lean in, not away from women's stories," said Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America. "As we move into the final six months of this 2024 election battle, we are producing ads featuring women who've been harmed by the Biden administration's radical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. And sharing stories from women who chose life, based on resources made available to them by the pro-life community."\par \par \uc2 The Biden campaign sent Zurawski and Joshua to battleground states where suburban women are likely to play a key role in deciding the election. At coffee shops in Wisconsin, they appeared before small, largely female audiences and shared their stories. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich449\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6735
