\par \par \uc2 Women gathered at a Milwaukee roundtable this month to hear from Kaitlyn Joshua and Amanda Zurawski. PHOTO: Caleb Alvarado for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 When Zurawski went into early labor at 18 weeks pregnant in August 2022, she was told the baby wouldn't survive. She said doctors told her they couldn't induce labor because it would be considered an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Texas bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with few exceptions. She waited three days, until she had developed an infection that turned to sepsis, a potentially life-threatening condition. She was then induced and delivered a stillborn baby girl, but had to spend days in intensive care.\par \par \uc2 "The damage that the sepsis caused has permanently compromised my fertility. So it's even harder for me to get pregnant again, if I can get pregnant again. I likely can't," said Zurawski, who described Willow as a "desperately wanted" baby.\par \par \uc2 Joshua, whose state of Louisiana also bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with few exceptions, was pregnant with her second child when she started experiencing pain and bleeding at 11 weeks. She said two hospitals wouldn't confirm that she was miscarrying, though her hormone levels were falling and it appeared the fetus had stopped growing. At the second hospital, she requested a surgical procedure to remove the fetus and was denied, she said. At a third medical visit, a midwife confirmed the miscarriage but also wouldn't perform the procedure, she said. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
