\par \par \uc2 The Biden campaign has deployed Kaitlyn Joshua to battlegrounds like Wisconsin, where suburban women are expected to play a key role in deciding the election. PHOTO: Caleb Alvarado for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Legal experts have said that restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws can make providers hesitant to provide certain types of miscarriage care. Joshua said she believes that happened to her. Two of the hospitals Joshua visited said they hadn't changed any practices due to state laws.\par \par \uc2 The hospital where Zurawski was treated and one other medical center Joshua visited didn't respond to emails seeking comment.\par \par \uc2 The two women's campaign stops blended trappings of traditional retail politicking\u8212\'20\'14Biden campaign signs and plugs to get out and vote\u8212\'20\'14with a confessional intimacy as attendees shared their experiences as well. Zurawski, in particular, was greeted with enthusiasm as many women knew her from the ad.\par \par \uc2 "That commercial has made me cry, I don't know how many times," 72-year-old Tina Kanetzke of Menomonee Falls, Wis., who plans to vote for Biden, told Zurawski at one event. "It's wonderful."\par \par \uc2 Zurawski answered: "It was not easy. So, thank you for that."\par \par \uc2 Write to Catherine Lucey at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:catherine.lucey@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 catherine.lucey@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240427ek4r000uo\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
