\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Weighs Emergency Abortions in States Where Procedure Is Banned; Justices consider doctor responsibilities when pregnant patients are experiencing health crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin \par \uc2 892 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par 21:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Supreme Court struggled Wednesday with the consequences of its 2022 decision {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ending the nationwide right to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , in a case pitting the health of women suffering medical emergencies against an Idaho law that bans the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The state asked the justices to overrule lower court orders allowing doctors to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/supreme-court-turns-to-emergency-abortions-as-dobbs-fallout-continues-9bfe8c9d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 perform emergency abortions}}}\uc2  when necessary to stabilize a patient. The Biden administration, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-department-files-lawsuit-challenging-idaho-abortion-law-11659463607"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which sued Idaho,}}}\uc2  argued that under a federal law governing emergency care, doctors can provide abortions they judge medically necessary to treat a woman experiencing a serious health scare.\par \par \uc2 Idaho's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law is stricter, permitting the procedure only to save a woman's life, and on Wednesday the state's lawyer, Joshua Turner, told the court that it was the state's prerogative to define the scope of permissible medical treatments.\par \par \uc2 Liberal justices challenged that view.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  said federal law imposed several duties on emergency rooms beyond state law. "So what that means is that when a hospital wants to only provide stabilizing care in emergencies for people who can pay for it," federal law "says, no, I'm sorry, you have to stabilize anyone who's experiencing an emergency medical condition," she said.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  said that meant states couldn't deny patients care that the medical profession considers necessary. Idaho couldn't say, "Don't treat diabetics with insulin. Treat them only with pills," said Sotomayor, who is diabetic. By the same token, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a treatment for some serious conditions, even if forgoing the procedure won't cause the woman to die, she said.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , a member of the court's conservative wing, suggested that the Idaho law wasn't as rigid as it might appear and that doctors could perform emergency abortions on patients who might be at future risk of dying but were yet to arrive at death's door.\par \par \uc2 As a condition of accepting Medicare funding, the federal Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act requires emergency rooms to treat people who show up in a medical crisis, including women in labor, to stabilize their condition. The administration argues that the federal law, known as Emtala, permits the procedure to preserve the patient's health, for instance, if needed to save the patient's reproductive organs.\par \par \uc2 Idaho argued that the administration's approach would "turn emergency rooms into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  enclaves," a misapplication of a 1986 law designed to prevent hospitals from dumping undesirable patients, such as those without insurance, onto other facilities.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  questioned how broad the federal government believed the health exception should extend.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Should hospitals be allowed to provide abortions in dire circumstances even in states that now ban the procedure? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 "Does the term 'health' mean just physical health or does it mean mental health?" Alito asked the administration's lawyer, Solicitor General \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Let me be very clear about our position," she said. A mental-health emergency "could never lead to pregnancy termination, because that is not the accepted standard of practice to treat" such conditions, she said.\par \par \uc2 In a tense exchange, Alito, who wrote the 2022 opinion overruling Roe v. Wade, suggested that Emtala itself rejected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as medical treatment.\par \par \uc2 In its definition of medical emergencies, the statute includes those that place "the health of the woman's unborn child in serious jeopardy," Alito said.\par \par \uc2 "It seems that the plain meaning is that the hospital must try to eliminate any immediate threat to the child," he said. "Performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is antithetical to that duty."\par \par \uc2 "The statute did nothing to displace the woman herself as an individual with an emergency medical condition," Prelogar replied.\par \par \uc2 Turner sought to assure the justices that in practice, little differed between the requirements of the state and federal laws.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , whose vote may be pivotal in deciding the case, asked whether doctors could be prosecuted for performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the state believed it wasn't necessary to save the woman's life.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors could choose to do so, Turner said, and courts would decide whether the doctor acted in good faith or used the life-saving exception as a pretext to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has permitted Idaho to enforce its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban while its appeal proceeds. Prelogar told the court that, as a result, several women with medical emergencies had been transported out of state to hospitals permitted to treat their conditions.\par \par \uc2 "Doctors either have to delay treatment and allow her condition to materially deteriorate, or they're airlifting her out of the state so she can get the emergency care that she needs," Prelogar said.\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have imposed near-total bans}}}\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Supreme Court overruled Roe v. Wade in its 2022 decision Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's case is one of two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters on the high court's docket. The justices are also considering a lawsuit that seeks to roll back approval for the widely used {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/abortion-pill-access-case-texas-judge-11651775689"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pill mifepristone}}}\uc2 . Decisions in both cases are expected by late June.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Jess.Bravin@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jess.Bravin@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240424ek4o0050l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
