\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Arizona House Votes to Repeal  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban; If passed by state Senate and signed into law, ban would still take effect for a few months before ending\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eliza Collins and Christine Mai-Duc \par \uc2 635 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par 21:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 PHOENIX\u8212\'20\'14Arizona's state House voted Wednesday to repeal a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that has become {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/arizona-abortion-poll-election-2024-def7bb14"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a hot-button political issue}}}\uc2  in the state and the nation.\par \par \uc2 The state Senate will vote on the measure next week, and several Republicans have signaled they would join Democrats to support it, giving it a good chance of passage. Democratic Gov. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  has said she would sign a repeal.\par \par \uc2 In a 32-28 vote, the GOP-controlled Arizona House voted to repeal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/arizona-republicans-repeal-abortion-ban-da903fa4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the 160-year-old law}}}\uc2  banning nearly all abortions that was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-ban-821501cb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reinstated earlier this month}}}\uc2  by the state Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Three Republicans joined with all the Democrats in the House to pass the measure.\par \par \uc2 "I'm thrilled the House has finally decided to do the right thing and repeal the archaic 1864 near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban," Hobbs wrote on X. "It's time for the Senate to follow suit and send the repeal to my desk. Immediately."\par \par \uc2 If the ban is repealed, it would still go into effect in June at the earliest and remain in effect for several months, because bills in Arizona don't typically take effect until 90 days after the legislative session ends. Once that period ends, an earlier Arizona law allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through 15 weeks of pregnancy would go back into effect.\par \par \uc2 "I refuse to buy into the false notion pushed by the extremes on both sides of this issue that we cannot respect and protect women and defend new life at the same time," said Rep. Matt Gress, a Republican who led the repeal effort Wednesday and represents a competitive House district.\par \par \uc2 Rep. Alexander Kolodin, a Republican who voted against the measure, said on the state House floor, "At the end of the day, politics is important but it is not worth our souls."\par \par \uc2 The Arizona Senate will take up the repeal on May 1, according to a spokeswoman for the chamber's GOP majority.\par \par \uc2 The near-total ban has given Democrats momentum in a battleground state. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Kari Lake, the expected GOP candidate for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , have called on the state legislature to repeal it, despite touting their antiabortion records in other circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of what ends up happening with the ban, Arizona voters are expected to consider a ballot measure in November that would expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability, or about halfway through a typical pregnancy. Democrats are hopeful that measure {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/biden-pushes-abortion-message-in-trumps-home-state-of-florida-024370bb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will help turn out voters}}}\uc2  for their candidates, as polling has shown a majority of Americans and Arizonans support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 In anticipation of Arizona's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban going into effect, California Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  unveiled an unusual proposal Wednesday that would allow doctors from Arizona to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services to their patients in California.\par \par \uc2 The bill, which likely will pass California's overwhelmingly Democratic legislature, is the latest move in a continuing political battle playing out between states over the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, which has taken center stage since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v. Wade}}}\uc2  in 2022. Newsom has been an outspoken opponent of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-restrictions-pass-across-u-s-as-republicans-overcome-divisions-331d7d31"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moves by Republicans to limit abortions}}}\uc2  and even spent campaign funds on ads in GOP-controlled states criticizing them.\par \par \uc2 Newsom and other state Democratic leaders have vowed to make California a "sanctuary" for those seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Newsom said the actions in Arizona's legislature Wednesday don't change his plans to push the California bill.\par \par \uc2 Alicia A. Caldwell contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Eliza Collins at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:eliza.collins@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliza.collins@wsj.com}}}\uc2  and Christine Mai-Duc at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:christine.maiduc@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 christine.maiduc@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240424ek4o00691\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
