\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Leaving  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  to the States Requires Federal Action; Trump would have to undo a series of 'workaround' regulations Biden imposed to evade the law.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rachel N. Morrison and Eric Kniffin \par \uc2 885 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2024\par 20:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Leave it to the states. That's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's new position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "My view," he said in an April 8 statement, "is now that we have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  where everybody wanted it from a legal standpoint, the states will determine by vote or legislation or perhaps both. And whatever they decide must be the law of the land\u8212\'20\'14in this case, the law of the state."\par \par \uc2 Some pro-life leaders criticized the statement. But setting aside the debate over federal pro-life laws, it's important to note that Mr. Trump's "law of the state" position still requires presidential action. That's because President Biden is using the executive branch unilaterally and unlawfully to impose his radical pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda on the American people, undermining pro-life states' ability to set their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. As a federal judge wrote last year, the Biden administration has "openly stated its intention to operate by fiat to find non-legislative workarounds to Supreme Court dictates," which amounts to "a breach of constitutional constraints."\par \par \uc2 A month after the Dobbs decision, the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2  warned hospitals that the federal Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, or Emtala\u8212\'20\'14which requires them to stabilize pregnant mothers and their unborn children\u8212\'20\'14could require physicians to perform and complete abortions. The following month the Justice Department sued Idaho, claiming Emtala pre-empted the state's pro-life law. Under this theory, many state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions would be unenforceable. (On Wednesday the Supreme Court will hear oral arguments in Moyle v. U.S., in which Idaho seeks to stay an injunction against the law's enforcement.)\par \par \uc2 Two days after its Emtala warning, \uc2 HHS\uc2  instructed pharmacies across the U.S. that federal nondiscrimination law requires them to stock and dispense \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs regardless of state laws. \uc2 HHS\uc2  retreated after Texas brought a lawsuit in February 2023 in federal court and received a favorable ruling. But we expect \uc2 HHS\uc2  to advance a similar theory in Affordable Care Act regulations, which will likely be finalized this spring.\par \par \uc2 The Veteran Affairs Department recently finalized a rule that allows its hospitals and clinics to provide abortions, even late-term abortions, where such procedures are prohibited by state law. The VA claimed that under the Constitution's Supremacy Clause, it isn't bound by any state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations, including "timeframe limitations, evidentiary requirements, or prerequisite procedures (such as mandatory waiting periods or required ultrasounds)." In effect, the rule converts the VA's health system\u8212\'20\'14with more than twice as many locations as \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14into the nation's largest network of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Justice Department's Office of Legal Counsel\uc2  rubber-stamped the regulation, claiming that the federal law prohibiting the VA from providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  benefits has been "effectively overtaken"\u8212\'20\'14a novel legal theory that has never been endorsed by any court.\par \par \uc2 On April 22, \uc2 HHS\uc2  finalized new privacy regulations for "reproductive healthcare" information that would prohibit the use or disclosure of health information that could be used to sue or prosecute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. The regulations would also create byzantine new procedures that doctors and hospitals would have to navigate before complying with subpoenas, court orders and other lawful requests for protected health information even "potentially related to reproductive healthcare." The regulations threaten doctors and hospitals with ruinous fines for cooperating with subpoenas, court orders and other legitimate efforts to enforce state pro-life laws.\par \par \uc2 Each of these post-Dobbs executive actions is a violation of federal law, bedrock principles of federalism, and every pro-life state's right to advance legitimate governmental interests, including those affirmed by the Supreme Court in Dobbs: "respect for and preservation of prenatal life at all stages of development; the protection of maternal health and safety; the elimination of particularly gruesome or barbaric medical procedures; the preservation of the integrity of the medical profession; the mitigation of fetal pain; and the prevention of discrimination on the basis of race, sex, or disability."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's promise to honor the "law of the state" marks a contrast with Mr. Biden's approach to executive power. Rolling back these Biden administration actions that have elevated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access above states' rights would reduce the power of the administrative state and free states to pursue their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. Undoing these executive actions is also a necessary step to achieve the pro-life movement's long-term goal of protecting and welcoming every human life.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's position would\u8212\'20\'14for the first time since Dobbs\u8212\'20\'14give states the freedom to foster a culture that respects the inherent dignity of each human life. Freeing states to enforce their pro-life laws without unlawful interference by the federal government, as Mr. Trump's position requires, would be a win for the pro-life cause.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Morrison directs the Ethics and Public Policy Center's HHS Accountability Project. She served as an attorney adviser at the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2 , 2019-21. Mr. Kniffin is a fellow at EPPC and a member of the \uc2 HHS\uc2  Accountability Project. He served as an attorney at the \uc2 Justice Department's Civil Rights Division\uc2 , 2005-09.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240423ek4n005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
