\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 New U.S. Rule Bans Release of Health Records in Investigations of Women Who Get Legal Abortions; Biden administration move adds to fight over how states can enforce  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  restrictions if women cross state lines or take  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephanie Armour \par \uc2 818 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2024\par 19:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A new rule from the Biden administration will strengthen privacy protections for people who obtain legal abortions, banning the release of their health records when they or their doctors are subject to investigation or prosecution.\par \par \uc2 The restrictions follow mounting concerns from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -access supporters that law enforcement in states that restrict or ban abortions will go after patients who obtain abortions in states without such limits or who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/health/healthcare/abortion-pill-case-puts-supreme-court-back-in-the-hot-seat-dec8edad"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 take \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pills  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It is the latest front in a fight unfolding {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in states  }}}\uc2  and in Washington following the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2022 U.S. Supreme Court decision  }}}\uc2  withdrawing constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Forty-one states have imposed some sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, while President Biden has wielded executive orders {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-administration-releases-new-hipaa-guidelines-in-light-of-abortion-ruling-11656540873"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 and regulation  }}}\uc2  to try to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a divided Congress where there is scant possibility of restoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 A congressional probe last year found that some of the country's largest pharmacy chains have provided prescription records to law enforcement without a warrant. Some states that ban most abortions except in cases of rape or incest require that law enforcement be notified in cases of sexual assault or child abuse.\par \par \uc2 The administration's push for stiffer privacy protections has already spurred the threat of lawsuits and heated criticism from Republicans who say it violates states' rights. Nineteen GOP attorneys general in July asked the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  to withdraw the proposal because they assert that it unlawfully interferes with states' authority to enforce their laws.\par \par \uc2 "The proposed rule exceeds the department's statutory authority," the July letter said. "HIPAA authorizes \uc2 HHS\uc2  to set standards for protecting privacy in 'health information.' The statute does not empower \uc2 HHS\uc2  to shield from authorities evidence of legal wrongdoing under state law based simply on a claimed connection to 'health care.'"\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 HHS\uc2  rule shores up the federal Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, or HIPAA, a 1996 federal law that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/your-health-data-isnt-as-safe-as-you-think-11574418606"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protects patient information  }}}\uc2  and establishes standards for its disclosure. The update makes it unlawful to disclose private medical data if it is being sought in connection with an investigation into a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or other reproductive care, even if a patient crosses state lines for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The move is an effort to protect providers, insurers and patients involved in lawful reproductive healthcare, according to \uc2 HHS\uc2  . The protections apply to prenatal care, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the management of miscarriages, contraception use, infertility treatment and other related conditions such as ovarian cancer.\par \par \uc2 "Under federal law, you have rights to your privacy," \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  said at a Monday press conference announcing the final rule. "That's what today is about. Making sure that Americans who convey very personal private health information have rights."\par \par \uc2 Fourteen states have total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  , a research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Twenty-seven states have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans based on gestational duration.\par \par \uc2 Biden already has signed orders to promote access to reproductive care, increase women's health research and expand access to contraception. But the medical privacy protections amount to one of Biden's most sweeping and polarizing actions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I'm shocked by how radical this is," said \uc2 Roger Severino\uc2  , an \uc2 HHS\uc2  official during the Trump administration who now serves as vice president at \uc2 the conservative Heritage Foundation\uc2  . "They say medical providers must disobey lawful warrants, state or federal. I've never heard such a thing from a regulation."\par \par \uc2 He said parents could be barred from getting \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  information about their children from providers.\par \par \uc2 Providers and patients have faced mounting concerns that they could face prosecution for providing or obtaining abortions. National and state groups that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month issued a statement saying they don't support legislation or resolutions that seek to criminalize or punish women who have abortions.\par \par \uc2 At least 61 individuals were criminally investigated or arrested for allegedly ending their own pregnancy or helping someone else to do so, according to an updated 2023 report from If/When/How, a national nonprofit network of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyers and law students. Almost 40% of the cases were reported to law enforcement by healthcare providers, according to the study.\par \par \uc2 Brittany Watts, an Ohio woman, was charged with abuse of a corpse in 2023 after undergoing a miscarriage at home and a hospital nurse called the police. Grand jurors in January {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/miscarriage-prosecution-ohio-brittany-watts-68145b3044b3cc61017b71a97f7cc036"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined to return an indictment  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lizelle Herrera was charged in 2022 with murder over an alleged self-induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was reported by a Texas hospital. The charges {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/health-texas-dallas-crime-arrests-ba0858c691744da84a90f2e38560dea3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were dropped  }}}\uc2 , and Herrera this year sued the Starr County prosecutor for bringing the charges.\par \par \uc2 Write to Stephanie Armour at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Stephanie.Armour@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stephanie.Armour@wsj.com  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240422ek4m004ed\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
