\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Trump Risks Losing Suburban Women Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 ; WSJ poll shows the issue is make-or-break for many of the swing-state voters who decided the last two elections and could again in 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Lucey in Waukesha, Wis. and Ken Thomas in Bloomfield Township, Mich. \par \uc2 1,805 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2024\par 01:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the most powerful issue driving suburban women who could decide the presidential election. Now President Biden is trying to harness that energy, while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is looking to redirect it.\par \par \uc2 A recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-biden-poll-swing-states-ad594acb?mod=hp_lead_pos7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wall Street Journal poll }}}\uc2  of seven battleground states found that 39% of suburban women cite \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a make-or-break issue {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/nikki-haley-primary-voters-donald-trump-support-election-82666934"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for their vote }}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14making it by far the most motivating issue for the group. Nearly three-quarters of them say the procedure should be legal all or most of the time, and a majority thinks Trump's policies are too restrictive.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been top of mind for voters since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2022 Supreme Court decision }}}\uc2  eliminating federal protections for the procedure, which unfurled a messy patchwork of state laws across the country.\par \par \uc2 That spotlight intensified in the past week, as Trump, the presumptive GOP nominee, said the issue {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/abortion-donald-trump-2024-election-fe641cc5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 should be left to the states }}}\uc2  but has taken murky or conflicting positions on some hot-button proposals. Then Arizona's highest court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-ban-821501cb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revived a Civil-War era ban }}}\uc2  on most abortions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/arizona-abortion-poll-election-2024-def7bb14"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heightening the stakes }}}\uc2  in a swing state where voters will likely cast ballots in November on a measure that would legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until viability.\par \par \uc2 Those moves are reverberating in the suburban areas where women helped deliver the White House to Trump in 2016 and swung it to Biden in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Longtime Wisconsin Democratic activist Dana Glasstein, 57, said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives her an extra sense of urgency as she knocks on doors in the Milwaukee suburbs to encourage people to vote. She had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  herself as a 19-year-old college student and expressed frustration that young women today may have less access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was safe, it was legal and I exercised my right," said the educator who lives in Mequon, Wis. "I have no regrets and the fact that I had that right allowed me to control the trajectory of my life."\par \par \uc2 Today, she said that women frequently share similar concerns with her. "When I ask what the issues are that would concern them and get them to the polls, they definitely mention women's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," she said.\par \par \uc2 Biden beat Trump nationally in 2020 among suburban women by 19 percentage points, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/graphics/votecast-2020/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the AP VoteCast survey }}}\uc2  of the electorate. The Journal's recent swing-state poll showed Biden leading by 17 points with that voter group in a two-way matchup, though roughly a third of suburban women were either undecided or hadn't fully committed to Trump or Biden.\par \par \uc2 Interviews with roughly two dozen women in key suburban areas in recent weeks found that many were motivated by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , naming it as a leading reason to back Biden. Still, the interviews showed that Biden faced the risk of having other issues crowd out \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as areas of concern, including the war in Gaza and inflation. And some of the women said they planned to stick with Trump.\par \par \uc2 Kelly Stacknick, 48, of Chandler, Ariz., called her state's ruling "infuriating," but said it may not drive her vote, expressing concerns about the cost of living. She voted for Biden in 2020, but said she was still considering Trump and Biden this time, though she would prefer other options. "Get these old guys that we've been dealing with for eight years out of here and get someone new," she said.\par \par \uc2 According to the Journal poll, 57% of suburban women thought Trump's policies on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were too restrictive. Just 20% said Biden's policies weren't restrictive enough. The survey was conducted by Democratic pollster Michael Bocian and Republican pollster Tony Fabrizio, who worked for a pro-Trump super PAC when the poll was conducted and has since joined the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 Biden has campaigned on a promise to restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and has used the issue to assail Trump, who as president appointed three justices to the court that overturned the 1973 Roe v. Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 Following the Arizona ruling, the president's campaign moved quickly. They pushed out an ad that blamed Trump for the struggles of a woman who couldn't get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas after receiving a likely fatal fetal diagnosis. They also pumped money on the Arizona airwaves with an ad featuring Biden promising to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaigned on this issue in Arizona Friday, saying: "Here's what a second Trump term looks like: more bans, more suffering, less freedom\u8230\'20\'26But we are not going to let that happen."\par \par \uc2 Trump, meanwhile, has struggled to find his footing on the issue. While he has repeatedly bragged about the 2022 decision, saying Friday, "We broke Roe v. Wade," he has also sought distance by saying decisions should be made by the states. But the Arizona ruling underscored the risks with that strategy: After initially saying it was a state issue, Trump later said the Arizona court had gone too far and that the governor and state legislature should "remedy what has happened."\par \par \uc2 Trump is also vulnerable to charges by the Biden campaign that by leaving it to states he is endorsing a six-week ban in Florida, where he lives and will be voting in the 2024 election, as well as other states throughout the South with effective bans. Trump said this week without evidence that "Florida's probably going to change" its policy, but didn't say what he thought the state's position should be.\par \par \uc2 Trump described himself as pro-choice in 1999, but said in 2015 he had "evolved" on the issue and later pledged to appoint antiabortion judges to the Supreme Court. As president, he endorsed a 20-week federal ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2018. As recently as last month, he suggested {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/trump-signals-support-15-week-abortion-ban-rcna144213"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he was open }}}\uc2  to a 15-week federal ban. On Thursday, he told reporters he wouldn't sign a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Trump spokeswoman Karoline Leavitt said Trump has long supported letting states decide on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said that immigration and inflation will remain top concerns of women across the country.\par \par \uc2 Trump's shifting positions have prompted some voters not to trust him on the issue.\par \par \uc2 "When Trump came out with his statement\u8230\'20\'26I just don't believe it. I am definitely still fearful that once the election goes through that he would be OK with them having a ban over the country and that my state rights would be taken away regardless," said Dr. Dana Rockwell, a 38-year-old rheumatologist and a Democrat from Royal Oak, Mich., who plans to vote for Biden.\par \par \uc2 While Trump will keep being asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the former president and his campaign are trying to pivot attention to issues like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/election-2024-immigration-issue-voters-84916a17"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 immigration }}}\uc2  and the economy, where polls show him with a clear advantage over Biden.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Do you think the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue will drive suburban women voters toward Biden? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 That focus is helping him maintain the support of voters like Michelle Hoppe, 61, of Fitchburg, Wis., who said she would be backing Trump and that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't a top issue for her. "When he was president, our economy was booming across all demographics. We had immigration under control," she said. "My 401(k) was going great."\par \par \uc2 Linda Sweets, 69, a Republican retiree from Waukesha, Wis., said she would also be voting for Trump though she didn't always like his rhetoric. On \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  she said, "I'm pro-life. I'm Catholic."\par \par \uc2 The laws governing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  vary by state, as do the politics. In Michigan, voters in 2022 approved an amendment enshrining the right in the state constitution. In Wisconsin the procedure is legal, but a court battle over whether an 1849 law should bar abortions continues.\par \par \uc2 Jessie Kelly, 34, a skilled trades worker for \uc2 General Motors\uc2  in Mount Clemens, Mich., and an independent, said she viewed Biden as more supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights than Trump. But she said she's considering not voting for president in the election\u8212\'20\'14leaving the top of her ballot blank\u8212\'20\'14because of Biden's handling of the war in Gaza, where tens of thousands of Palestinians have been killed. And she expressed frustration in seemingly having to choose between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 "What I think is happening inside of Israel and Palestine right now, I believe, is genocide. So if I go in there and I check that box, am I complicit in saying that it's OK to use my tax dollars for genocide? I can't do that," Kelly said.\par \par \uc2 Erin McManaway, 28, an independent from West Allis, Wis., said she voted "unaffiliated" in the Democratic primary in the state over her concerns about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/bidens-approach-to-israel-gaza-conflict-angers-both-sides-46fa0280"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden's Israel policy }}}\uc2 . "He needs to get it together when it comes to Gaza," she said. "The fact that Biden is still sending weapons to them is unacceptable."\par \par \uc2 But McManaway said she would vote for Biden over Trump in November, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a leading reason. She said that shortly after the Dobbs ruling, "I had to drive a friend to Illinois to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She was in a situation when having a child wasn't feasible."\par \par \uc2 Some said they were considering alternatives to Trump or Biden. Rachael Jurek, 47, of Whitefish Bay, Wis., said she might write in Rep. \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  (D., Minn.), who launched an unsuccessful primary bid against Biden. "I feel the Democrats, if they wanted my vote, they would have listened to me," said Jurek, who described herself as an independent. She stressed that she feels "there should be safeguards in place for women to get the healthcare they need."\par \par \uc2 One concern for some Democrats is maintaining energy on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, following years of fights\u8212\'20\'14and years of activism around the Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 Lori Goldman, 65, a Democratic activist in Michigan's Oakland County who founded an organization called Fems for Dems, said many of her allies are in "battle fatigue" after a spirited effort to win support for the 2022 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment. While she said many Democrats are "waking up" to the threat of a potential national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, they are still struggling to show the urgency throughout the state.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is like the hydra, you know? You cut off a head, he grows another one. That's what we're battling," Goldman said. "It's going to take us all. We have to keep going. We can't allow fatigue or disillusionment to stop us."\par \par \uc2 Write to Catherine Lucey at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:catherine.lucey@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 catherine.lucey@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Ken Thomas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:ken.thomas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ken.thomas@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240413ek4d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
