\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Why Arizona Will Be Ground Zero for the 2024  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Fight;  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  is a major issue for Arizona voters, WSJ poll shows; GOP on defense\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eliza Collins \par \uc2 1,368 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2024\par 17:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 PHOENIX\u8212\'20\'14Meagan Quattlebaum of Tucson said she was so horrified by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/arizona-republicans-repeal-abortion-ban-da903fa4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona Supreme Court's decision }}}\uc2  to ban nearly all abortions in her state that she now plans to volunteer to help Democrats win in November.\par \par \uc2 "I think it's a really selfish and ridiculous ruling," said Quattlebaum, 28, a registered independent who works in the healthcare industry. "It definitely influences the way I will be voting."\par \par \uc2 The Arizona Supreme Court's decision Tuesday to bring back a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/arizona-supreme-court-abortion-ban-821501cb?mod=hp_lead_pos1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 160-year-old near total ban }}}\uc2  on the procedure inflamed the politics in one of the nation's biggest electoral battlegrounds, which is now effectively ground zero for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics. With a tossup Senate race, a pair of close congressional seats and its 11 Electoral College votes on the line, the state will be one of the most closely watched in the country.\par \par \uc2 Arizona voters were most likely to say that they wouldn't support a candidate they disagreed with on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-biden-poll-swing-states-ad594acb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wall Street Journal poll }}}\uc2  conducted in March of seven swing states. In the poll, which was conducted before the Arizona ruling, the majority of voters there said they supported access to the procedure\u8212\'20\'14and more voters said they backed President Biden's approach than that of the presumptive GOP presidential candidate, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Across the seven states\u8212\'20\'14Nevada, Pennsylvania, Michigan, Wisconsin, Georgia and North Carolina in addition to Arizona\u8212\'20\'14\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the only issue where Biden had a clear advantage over his predecessor.\par \par \uc2 Biden won Arizona in 2020 by the narrowest margin in the country\u8212\'20\'14just over 10,000 votes. This year Biden lags behind Trump in most battleground and national polls, including the Journal poll of Arizona, in which he trailed the former president by 5 percentage points. Voters in Arizona have given Trump higher marks than Biden on issues such as the economy and immigration, which they say are their top concerns even ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While Democrats fretted about the practical and health consequences of the new ban, the decision was a political gift to a party eager to paint Republicans as extreme on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and responsible for the chaos that has followed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fall of Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2 , the Supreme Court decision that had established the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the ruling quickly put Republicans on defense.\par \par \uc2 "If past is prologue, this is going to have an immediate and lasting impact on politics in Arizona, and they bit off more than they can chew," said Kris Mayes, the Democratic attorney general. She credited her razor-thin victory in 2022\u8212\'20\'14by 280 votes\u8212\'20\'14to the fall of Roe earlier that year.\par \par \uc2 Arizona Democrats also said the ban could help them finally wrest control of the state Legislature, where Republicans hold narrow majorities in both chambers.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/why-independent-voters-broke-for-democrats-in-the-midterms-11668249002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  was a key issue }}}\uc2  in the 2022 midterm elections and helped Democrats to victory in races in Arizona and across the country.\par \par \uc2 Mayes and other Democrats have won the state in recent years by engaging Democratic voters and outperforming with independents and moderate Republicans. The Journal poll of Arizona voters showed that nine out of 10 Democrats, two-thirds of independents and one-third of Republicans support access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What impact will the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue have on Arizona voters in 2024? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 A ballot measure that would legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until viability\u8212\'20\'14or more than halfway through a typical pregnancy\u8212\'20\'14is expected to be put before voters in Arizona in November, giving Democrats hope they can bring out their coalition.\par \par \uc2 "I think we're going to see moderate Republican women and independents respond to this very sternly," said Jen Cox, senior adviser to the Biden campaign in Arizona. Biden has promised to restore access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and has criticized Trump for appointing the Supreme Court justices who were part of the majority that overturned the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Biden campaign is running two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -focused ads in Arizona, both of which blame Trump for restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Trump spokeswoman Karoline Leavitt said after the ruling: "President Trump could not have been more clear. These are decisions for people of each state to make." Trump on Monday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/abortion-donald-trump-2024-election-fe641cc5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined to take a national stance }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Arizona Republicans sought distance from the decision Tuesday as Democrats amped up their offensive efforts.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake, the expected GOP candidate in the state's highly competitive Senate race, was among the Republicans on Tuesday who said they opposed the new ruling and called on state lawmakers and Gov. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14a Democrat\u8212\'20\'14to come up with a new solution. During her unsuccessful bid for governor in 2022, Lake praised the 1864 ban but later softened her stance and told the Journal in the fall that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/kari-lake-to-announce-senate-run-setting-up-high-stakes-arizona-race-30ff1594"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she supported a 15-week ban }}}\uc2  and opposed a national ban.\par \par \uc2 Trump on Wednesday said the Arizona decision goes too far. "That'll be straightened out," he told reporters as he campaigned in Georgia. "And I'm sure that the governor and everybody else are going bring it back into reason, and that will be taken care of, I think, very quickly."\par \par \uc2 Hobbs called on the Legislature to repeal the 1864 ban, which would take the state back to the 15-week ban that had previously been in place. While Democrats say that law is still too restrictive, it would give some breathing room before voters can cast ballots in November on even-looser restrictions.\par \par \uc2 After Arizona Republicans squashed an effort Wednesday to move forward with a repeal of the law, the House Speaker issued a statement saying the legislature was reviewing options but didn't commit to holding a vote.\par \par \uc2 The Arizona Supreme Court, in a 4-2 decision, agreed that the 19th-century law takes precedence over the 15-week ban, which was passed in 2022. The court delayed implementation of the ban for at least two weeks to allow for additional legal arguments, but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates appear to have few options to prevent it from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 One potential benefit for Democrats, especially Biden, is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling could push disaffected Democrats to turn out. Biden has been facing a lack of enthusiasm from young voters, in part because of his handling of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/bidens-approach-to-israel-gaza-conflict-angers-both-sides-46fa0280"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 war between Israel and Hamas }}}\uc2  and the humanitarian crisis in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 "It's incredibly disheartening and angering, and now I'm nervous to live here," said Liz Hertel, 26, who does nonprofit work and lives in Phoenix, about the ban. Hertel, a Democrat, has been unhappy with Biden's support for Israel and foreign policy in general but will vote for him, Democrats and the ballot initiative in November.\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans acknowledged that the ballot measure was likely to pass but added that voters could still split their tickets.\par \par \uc2 "I do think that voters are discerning enough that they can vote for a ballot initiative and then vote for candidates based on the totality of the issues, including the border crisis and inflation," said Jon Seaton, a Republican strategist who has worked in the state. Seaton noted that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/election-day-ballots-ohio-abortion-maine-texas-colorado-d9922480"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ohio voters enshrined \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access }}}\uc2  into the state constitution, but are also expected to vote for Trump in November and have a highly competitive Senate race.\par \par \uc2 Dorothy Self, a 41-year-old independent who lives in Phoenix, is one of those voters. Self, who works in the medical field, said the ballot measure goes too far. But she feels strongly about the need for women to have choices\u8212\'20\'14she said she got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after being taken advantage of when she was younger\u8212\'20\'14and would vote in favor of a ballot measure this fall because of her support for exceptions for rape, incest, life of the mother and fetal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 "I would have to go with the one that gave more options to victims," she said. At the same time, she plans to vote for Republicans, including Trump, this fall.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Lucey, Laura Kusisto and Alex Leary contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Eliza Collins at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:eliza.collins@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliza.collins@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240410ek4a001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
