\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Democrats Get Personal on IVF, Putting Republicans on Defensive; In Iowa House contest, Democratic challenger \uc2 Christina Bohannan\uc2  highlights her story in pitch to voters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalie Andrews \par \uc2 1,308 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 March 2024\par 16:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DEWITT, Iowa\u8212\'20\'14A congressional race in Iowa is testing whether the national uproar surrounding in vitro fertilization will help Democrats win in November\u8212\'20\'14and potentially tip control of the House in their favor.\par \par \uc2 In the state's First Congressional District, Democrat \uc2 Christina Bohannan\uc2  is challenging Republican Rep. \uc2 Mariannette Miller-Meeks\uc2  in a rematch from 2022. This time, Bohannan is talking with voters about her personal experience with IVF, leaning into the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/alabama-supreme-court-embryo-ivf-uncertainty-88cf2146"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled in February}}}\uc2  that frozen embryos can be legally considered children, raising concerns about the future of fertility treatments in that state.\par \par \uc2 "When I heard the Alabama ruling, it just made me really angry, that these politicians, or in that case, the judges, would just take the opportunity away," said Bohannan, 52 years old, a law professor at the \uc2 University of Iowa\uc2 . She said she went through several rounds of IVF, which were unsuccessful, to try to have a second child.\par \par \uc2 Democrats think the battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  will shift their voters in their direction the same way {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/florida-abortion-rulings-to-keep-issue-front-and-center-in-november-1168511c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  boosted the party}}}\uc2  in 2022 after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court ruled there was no constitutional right}}}\uc2  to the procedure. While Republicans won control of the House that election, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/frustrated-republicans-try-to-explain-lack-of-midterm-red-wave-11668036382"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 their "red wave" failed to materialize}}}\uc2 . The unexpectedly narrow majority they were left with\u8212\'20\'14now 218-213\u8212\'20\'14has undercut their efforts to govern.\par \par \uc2 By raising the prospect of new restrictions on IVF, the Alabama ruling drew attention to the stance held by many Republicans that life begins at conception. That position, intended as an argument for banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also implicates IVF, in which some unused embryos are discarded.\par \par \uc2 Since the ruling, top anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republicans have backed access to IVF, while also saying the destruction of embryos raises ethical questions. After the court ruling in Alabama, the state's GOP-led legislature passed a law to shield providers from prosecution if embryos are destroyed during IVF treatments.\par \par \uc2 Now, the issue is playing out in red-leaning Iowa. A state law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went into effect last year}}}\uc2 , but since has been temporary blocked in court. Abortions are currently allowed up to 21 weeks and six days. The Iowa House in recent weeks has passed a bill that would criminalize the death of an "unborn person." Fertility-treatment advocates say the measure would limit IVF in the state.\par \par \uc2 Miller-Meeks, a physician, has a solidly conservative record and spent more than two decades in the Army, retiring as a lieutenant colonel. She is an opponent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with few exceptions, while saying she supports the use of IVF.\par \par \uc2 "I talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the same way I have since the first time I ran for office, and that is that I'm pro-life," she said. In the previous session of Congress, Miller-Meeks, 68, signed on to a bill stating that there is a right to life "including the moment of fertilization, cloning, or other moment at which an individual comes into being."\par \par \uc2 Opponents of the bill, and people involved in assisted reproductive technology, say the proposed legislation would affect fertility treatments and research. Miller-Meeks hasn't signed on to the bill in this Congress. She said that there was "no particular reason" for not backing it this time and added that she has some concerns about the ethics around IVF.\par \par \uc2 Republican leaders, sensitive to the way the party handled the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate in 2022, have told their members to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/republicans-new-abortion-strategy-talk-about-it-more-not-less-d68d80ef"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 talk about their positions}}}\uc2 , particularly in swing districts. They label Democrats as extreme for not making clear at what point in pregnancy they think an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be off limits. Democrats counter that they think it is a decision between a woman and her doctor, with House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  (D., N.Y.) charging that Republicans want to "criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and IVF."\par \par \uc2 Bohannan is trying to reverse a steady rightward shift in Iowa, where both senators and all four House representatives are Republicans. Miller-Meeks's seat is one of two in the state considered competitive, and nonpartisan forecaster Cook Political Report rates the race as "likely Republican." In 2022, Miller-Meeks beat Bohannan by more than 6 percentage points. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
