\par \par \uc2 Pat Henricksen, a registered Republican, in purple turtleneck, attended a gathering for \uc2 Christina Bohannan\uc2 , a Democrat running for Congress. PHOTO: KC McGinnis for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Over spicy deer sausage and sweet scotcheroos on Tuesday evening at a DeWitt house party, Bohannan asked the roughly 40 people who were there for their help courting voters. DeWitt is in Clinton County, which picked Democrats for president for decades until 2016, when Republican \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the county by 5 percentage points. In 2020, he won it by 11 points.\par \par \uc2 "What we have right now in this district is someone who is not there to represent Iowa," Bohannan said of Miller-Meeks. "She went to Congress and signed onto a life-at-conception bill," she said. "This is way too extreme."\par \par \uc2 Among those at the party was Pat Henricksen, 80, a retired educator and registered Republican. She has soured on Trump after backing him in 2020, but she's no fan of President Biden, either: "Nobody my age should be president." She voted for Miller-Meeks in 2022 and is now reconsidering.\par \par \uc2 "I'm a Catholic and you know, I myself wouldn't have had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I don't think," she said, but added: "I think every woman needs to have a choice." She said she would support an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How do you see the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue influencing voters in Iowa and beyond this year? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/WSJ_Partial_Results_Feb_2024.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wall Street Journal poll}}}\uc2  from earlier this year, voters of both parties ranked \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the top issue on which they must agree with a candidate. When asked to name the one issue on which they wouldn't vote for a candidate who disagreed with them, 22% said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The second-most-cited issue, at 20%, was immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 At a cafe on Thursday in DeWitt, Becca Leykamm, 38, a lab technician who lives in town, said the decision in Alabama made her angry, and that choosing a candidate that backs \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will be a "huge influence on my decision."\par \par \uc2 Candy Faust, 84, a retired real-estate worker, said she backed Trump in 2020 and 2016. She staunchly opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said she wouldn't back a candidate who supports it. Of people who use IVF, she said: "I think they need to leave God in charge." \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal7200
