\par \par \uc2 Candy Faust said she opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and won't back a candidate who supports it. PHOTO: Natalie Andrews/The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans facing tough races across the country have tried to trumpet their support for IVF. Republican Reps. Juan Ciscomani of Arizona, Lori Chavez-DeRemer of Oregon and Michelle Steel of California introduced a nonbinding resolution supporting protection for IVF. Steel also took her name off the current bill that states life begins at conception, stating "it could create confusion about my support for the blessings of having children through IVF."\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the issue, four House Republicans sent a letter to the Department of Veterans Affairs opposing the agency's expansion of access to in vitro fertilization, saying "IVF is morally dubious and should not be subsidized by the American taxpayer."\par \par \uc2 Such letters opposing IVF might not be helpful for Republicans in close races. Of the letter, Miller-Meeks said: "Does it make it more challenging? Yes. But our job is to represent the districts we're in."\par \par \uc2 Write to Natalie Andrews at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:natalie.andrews@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 natalie.andrews@wsj.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240330ek3u0015p\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
