\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Legal 'Standing' and the Mifepristone  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill Case; Liberals suddenly find virtue in the consistency of Supreme Court conservative Justices.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 611 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2024\par 21:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court Tuesday sounded skeptical about the challenge by pro-life doctors to the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's dispensing rules on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone. We confess to being amused that our friends on the left have suddenly discovered the importance of the Court's legal-standing principles.\par \par \uc2 The oral argument in FDA v. Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine focused largely on whether doctors could challenge the FDA's relaxation of mifepristone dispensing rules in federal court. As in all cases, plaintiffs must demonstrate legal standing, meaning a concrete injury that is traceable to a defendant's conduct and redressable by courts.\par \par \uc2 Under questioning, the plaintiffs couldn't identify a single doctor tangibly harmed by the FDA changes. Plaintiffs' attorney Erin Hawley argued that the FDA's rules might result in more adverse side effects that might result in more emergency-room visits that might result in doctors having to complete abortions that violate their consciences.\par \par \uc2 But as Solicitor General \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2  noted, federal law protects doctors from having to do so. One doctor attested that she was forced to perform a "dilation and curettage procedure" for a woman suffering severe complications.\par \par \uc2 But as Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  noted, that "does not necessarily mean that there was a living embryo or a fetus." She added that "the difficulty here is that, at least to me, these affidavits do read more like the conscience objection is strictly to actually participating in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end the life of the embryo or fetus," but the doctors don't seem to have "ever participated in that."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hawley also argued that her group has "been forced to divert resources from speaking and advocating for their pro-life mission generally to explaining the dangers of the harm from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs." But as Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  rejoined, an injury caused "simply by using resources to advocate their position in court" seems "easy to manufacture."\par \par \uc2 Were the Court to decide the plaintiffs have standing, Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  wondered why it shouldn't issue a narrow remedy to protect the conscience objectors instead of blocking the FDA rule changes nationwide.\par \par \uc2 "Normally, we would allow equitable relief to address them," Justice Gorsuch noted. Recently, "we've had one might call it a rash of universal injunctions or vacaturs. And this case seems like a prime example of turning what could be a small lawsuit into a nationwide legislative assembly on . . . an FDA rule or any other federal government action." He's right.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  was more sympathetic to the plaintiffs' claims, in part because he has criticized the Court's inconsistent enforcement of its standing principles. He noted that a "chain of probabilities" was sufficient to determine standing in the Department of Commerce v. New York (2019) case about the census questionnaire. He has a point and was in partial dissent in that case, but one standing mistake doesn't justify a bigger one.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito also raised concerns with Ms. Prelogar's claim that nobody would have standing to challenge the FDA's rule changes. That's not clear, but Ms. Prelogar correctly noted that the tort system remains available to plaintiffs to remedy adverse effects.\par \par \uc2 The conservative Justices have often been lambasted by their colleagues and the press for strictly enforcing the Court's standing principles, such as a challenge to Texas's so-called bounty-hunter \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law. It's nice to see that their critics now agree with them, at least when it suits the critics' policy preferences.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240326ek3q006pp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
