\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court and Mifepristone; The question in a case about the  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pill is whether judges can act as a Super \uc2 FDA\uc2  on drug approvals.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 820 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2024\par 20:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court on Tuesday will review the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's rules for dispensing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone. The main question in \uc2 FDA\uc2  v. Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine isn't whether regulators exceeded their authority, but whether lower-court judges have done so.\par \par \uc2 Doctors opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  argue that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  improperly eased its restrictions on administering mifepristone. In 2016 the agency allowed nurse practitioners, not only doctors, to prescribe the medication and increased the gestational age cutoff to 10 weeks from seven. Five years later the agency suspended its in-person requirement for dispensing the drug.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs claim that between 2.9% and 4.6% of women who take the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug end up in the emergency room, and that the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's dispensing changes have increased the risk of adverse events. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  violated the Administrative Procedure Act (APA), they argue, by not properly evaluating the cumulative increased safety risks of its changes.\par \par \uc2 FDA\uc2  disagrees. It says many women who use the drug seek care at the ER for cramping and bleeding, both expected results of the pill, and that studies show that hospitalization occurs in fewer than 0.7% of cases. The agency says it reviewed dozens of studies about the drug's safety with different dispensing regimens.\par \par \uc2 Merits aside, the threshold question for the Court is whether the doctors have legal standing to sue, which requires a concrete injury that is traceable to a defendant's actions and that can be redressed by courts. The plaintiffs don't appear to satisfy these requirements.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals held that doctors had standing because they might be forced to treat women experiencing complications in the emergency room, which would violate their conscience. The court also held that they might have to divert time and resources from regular patients and face greater liability and higher insurance costs.\par \par \uc2 But the plaintiffs can't show how the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's changes to its dispensing rules would lead to these supposed harms. How does letting a nurse practitioner rather than a doctor dispense the pill increase the likelihood that a woman will need emergency care? The risks of injury that plaintiffs claim they would suffer are speculative.\par \par \uc2 As for conscience violations, the doctors can't show they would be forced to complete abortions. Nor how treating women with cramping would offend their conscience. If the \uc2 FDA\uc2  rule changes were enjoined, doctors would still see women experiencing side effects from the pill, so it's also not clear how courts could redress their alleged injuries.\par \par \uc2 It's true the High Court has sometimes loosely applied its standing principles. The plaintiffs cite the Court's Department of Commerce v. New York decision (2019) in which courts allowed states to challenge a citizenship question on the Census based on a statistical probability they might be harmed by a population undercount.\par \par \uc2 But the pro-life doctors' arguments for standing would eviscerate any limiting principle. Doctors could bring lawsuits challenging \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of any drug that causes side effects that might result in ER visits. They could claim conscience protections to sue to overturn \uc2 FDA\uc2  approvals of drugs developed with stem-cell research.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Congress granted the \uc2 FDA\uc2  authority to review drug safety and efficacy. While the plaintiffs and lower courts quibble with how the agency evaluated scientific evidence, the job of judges isn't to serve as a Super FDA. "Courts must be careful not to unduly second-guess an agency's scientific judgment," Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  wrote while on the D.C. Circuit Court of Appeals (\uc2 Cytori Therapeutics\uc2  v. \uc2 FDA\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Pro-life doctors say the \uc2 FDA\uc2  dismissed the risk that women might mistake the drug's side effects for life-threatening undiagnosed ectopic pregnancies. But the \uc2 FDA\uc2  says mifepristone doesn't exacerbate this condition, which its review found occurs in only 0.005% of women prescribed the drug. Ectopic pregnancies can also be missed by ultrasounds, and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  isn't required to eliminate the risk of every potential adverse event.\par \par \uc2 Agency actions must merely be "reasonable and reasonably explained for purposes of the APA's deferential arbitrary-and-capricious standard," the High Court said in Prometheus Radio Project (2021). Agencies often don't have "perfect empirical or statistical data," and the APA "imposes no general obligation on agencies to conduct or commission" their own studies.\par \par \uc2 Yet that is essentially what the plaintiffs want the Court to force the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to do. Judicial flyspecking of government environmental reviews is a growing separation-of-powers problem that has stalled beneficial projects. If courts start second-guessing \uc2 FDA\uc2  decisions, it will slow the development of life-saving treatments. This is a case that calls for judicial modesty, no matter your views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240325ek3p003s5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{