\par\par \uc2 Life Science\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  and America's Phony 'Pregnancy Crisis'; Contrary to the CDC's claims, maternal mortality rates haven't changed much since 1999.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Allysia Finley \par \uc2 831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 March 2024\par 20:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. has a "pregnancy crisis," according to liberal medical experts and the press. They're referring to America's supposedly soaring maternal mortality, not its declining fertility.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. stands out "among high-income nations for its alarming incidence of maternal deaths despite substantial health care spending," the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  says. The group, like other activists, invokes U.S. maternal mortality to advocate expanded government welfare programs and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 "Evidence and experience show us conclusively that the risk of death during or after childbirth is approximately 14 times greater than the risk of death from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related complications," the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ama-assn.org/about/leadership/dobbs-ruling-assault-reproductive-health-safe-medical-practice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AMA says }}}\uc2 . Democratic states echo this claim in a friend-of-the-court brief in \uc2 FDA\uc2  v. Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, which the Supreme Court will hear Tuesday. Justices who were about to overturn Roe v. Wade would have " {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(22)00870-4/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blood on their hands }}}\uc2 ," the medical journal Lancet warned in a May 12, 2022, editorial.\par \par \uc2 As with the Covid pandemic, experts are using bad data to drive a political agenda. A new study this month in the American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology shows that oft-cited U.S. maternal-mortality statistics are inflated owing to discrepancies in how pregnancy deaths are recorded.\par \par \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention's National Vital Statistics System reports that maternal-mortality rates in the U.S. have roughly tripled since 2001, to 32.9 per 100,000 live births in 2021. This is nearly three times as high as rates in other developed countries\u8212\'20\'14but, as the study concludes, it's largely a statistical artifact.\par \par \uc2 Deaths among pregnant women or new mothers are often classified as "maternal" even if they owe to other causes, such as cancer or pre-existing conditions. The culprit is a check box that states added to death certificates in 2003 to identify women who had died while pregnant or between 42 days and a year of when their pregnancy ended.\par \par \uc2 As the study explains, this check box "led to a rapid increase in reported maternal mortality rates" and "some egregious errors," including hundreds of women over 70 "being certified as pregnant at the time of death or in the year before death" largely because of administrative errors.\par \par \uc2 Researchers reanalyzed mortality data to identify only deaths that occurred during pregnancy or postpartum that had at least one mention of pregnancy among the causes of death on the certificate. The authors found that the maternal mortality rate remained essentially flat between 1999 and 2002 (10.2 per 100,000 live births) and 2018 and 2021 (10.4). This would put the U.S. on par with other developed countries.\par \par \uc2 The study also found that deaths directly related to labor and pregnancy have fallen over time, suggesting medical care has improved. But indirect deaths resulting from pre-existing conditions that might have been aggravated by pregnancy\u8212\'20\'14such as hypertension and diabetes\u8212\'20\'14increased. This isn't surprising given the rise in obesity and related conditions.\par \par \uc2 Pregnancy-associated deaths\u8212\'20\'14i.e., those among pregnant women or new mothers from incidental causes\u8212\'20\'14also soared to 32.6 from 0.53 per 100,000 live births during the 20-year period. In other words, death rates for pregnant women and new mothers have shot up mainly because of other causes, especially drug overdoses.\par \par \uc2 The data also suggest that young women are no more likely to die from pregnancy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/datacenter/all-cause-mortality-rate-100000-women-ages-15-44"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 than they are from other causes }}}\uc2 . Bringing a new life into the world carries medical risks, but so does getting out of bed each day.\par \par \uc2 It isn't clear how expanding welfare would reduce deaths among pregnant women, which have increased amid the expansion of programs like Medicaid and food stamps. Overplaying the dangers of pregnancy may discourage some women from becoming mothers and cause some to terminate pregnancies they might not otherwise.\par \par \uc2 Comparing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pregnancy deaths is also like comparing apples and bananas. For one thing, federal law doesn't require states to report \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related complications. Deaths that result from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are often classified on death certificates as pregnancy-related. This isn't to say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unsafe, but data don't allow direct safety comparisons to pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Whatever your views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , claims that restricting it will cause maternal mortality to increase\u8212\'20\'14a 2021 study by \uc2 University of Colorado\uc2  researchers projected that banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide would lead to a 21% increase in "pregnancy-related deaths"\u8212\'20\'14are unfounded because data on such deaths are grossly inflated.\par \par \uc2 In any case, comparing the process of bringing a new life into the world to terminating one may strike many Americans as morally offensive. When progressives can't persuade the public, they invoke questionable science and try to get courts to impose the policies they favor. The result is a crisis of credibility for the liberal public-health establishment.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240324ek3o001rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
