\par\par \uc2 Main Street\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden's 'Unease' on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 ; He has abandoned the limits he supported as a senator\u8212\'20\'14and it isn't enough for advocates.\b0\par\par\uc2 By William McGurn \par \uc2 867 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2024\par 20:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  just made history. On Thursday she became the first vice president to visit an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, stopping by a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  location in Minnesota to tout the Biden administration's proud support. Probably it also marked the first time Ms. Harris was the administration's voice of clarity.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is President Biden's top issue going into the November election. As he has made clear, there isn't a single \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  he would restrict, and he opposes the Hyde Amendment's limits on using tax dollars to pay for them. In political terms, there is no more absolute pro-choice stance.\par \par \uc2 Yet amid all the headlines about Republican extremism, no one ever points that out. Except Megan Crowley. She did so in an article for RealClearPolicy that ran under the headline "Dear Mr. President, My Life Is Worth Living."\par \par \uc2 She brought up Kate Cox, a Texas woman the president invoked at the State of the Union. Ms. Cox sought an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after her unborn child was diagnosed with Trisomy 18. Doctors told her that the child wouldn't live long even if born, and that Ms. Cox might not be able to get pregnant again if she carried the baby to term.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Crowley acknowledged Ms. Cox's predicament was heartbreaking. But she lamented that Mr. Biden's empathy was stunted by the assumption that "Kate's child\u8212\'20\'14and any child diagnosed with Trisomy 18\u8212\'20\'14could never live a happy life."\par \par \uc2 Echoing Hubert Humphrey's line that the test of government is how it treats people in the dawn of life, the twilight of life and the shadows of life, Ms. Crowley puts it this way in an email:\par \par \uc2 "The mark of a great society is what it does to protect the weakest and most vulnerable among us," she writes. "Absent from this \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate is any explicit acknowledgment of the value and dignity of every person born with a genetic disease or a disability. We deserve a chance. We are part of the richness of a truly diverse and inclusive society. Our lives are worthy of birth, and worthy of living."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Crowley was diagnosed at 15 months with a genetic disorder called Pompe disease, and doctors told her parents to go home and enjoy what little time they would have with her. Instead, her father founded a biotech company to come up with a cure for Megan and her brother, Patrick, a story told in the 2010 film "Extraordinary Measures" starring Harrison Ford. Ms. Crowley is now 27. Though reliant on a wheelchair, ventilator and feeding tube, she lives a full and happy life, including graduating from Notre Dame and holding down a job.\par \par \uc2 In today's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, America's Megan Crowleys are all but invisible. Because acknowledging the complexity of another human life is at odds with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  priorities. These priorities have shifted in the two years since Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, in which the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, the landmark 1973 ruling that legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Not content with recent victories at the polls, some Democrats now push party leaders to speak of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a positive as opposed to a sad and regrettable necessity.\par \par \uc2 These activists aren't happy with Joe Biden. Notwithstanding his policies, Politico, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  and the Associated Press each ran stories noting what the Post called his "skittishness" on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14especially about using the word. At the State of the Union, for example, instead of reading "because Texas law banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 " as it appeared in his prepared text, he ad-libbed "because Texas law banned her ability to act."\par \par \uc2 "By not saying the word '\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' it implies that it's taboo or something to be ashamed of," Kellie Copeland, executive director of Pro-Choice Ohio, told the AP. "It's stigmatizing and harmful. The president should do better."\par \par \uc2 How far we are from \uc2 Mario Cuomo\uc2 , creator of the "personally opposed" dodge that pro-choice Catholic Democrats used for years. Or even \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's "safe, legal and rare," which his wife, Hillary, walked away from in her 2016 run for president. Now an 81-year-old Catholic president has repudiated every limit on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  he once voted for as a senator to do the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lobby's bidding\u8212\'20\'14and it still isn't enough.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps Mr. Biden's critics are right. Maybe the American people have overcome the qualms they once had about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Certainly that's one read of the post-Dobbs electoral results. It explains why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is talking about moderating the GOP position.\par \par \uc2 Plainly the politics are shifting, and Mr. Biden is less cause than symptom. He is where he is on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the same reason he is on any issue\u8212\'20\'14because he's drifted with his party. But where Democratic leaders once thought they needed Cuomo- and Clinton-like nuance and euphemism, the generation exemplified by Ms. Harris are more honest about what it is they really want: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand.\par \par \uc2 Write to mcgurn@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240318ek3i00565\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
