\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 House Republicans' 2024 Strategy: Talk About  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  More, Not Less; Republicans should stress empathy for women and discuss 'common sense' solutions, memo says\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalie Andrews \par \uc2 1,270 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par 00:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 An earlier version of this story said that Florida has banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks. While the state has passed a law to do so, it is tied up in court. (Corrected on March 28)\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://asset.wsj.net/wsjnewsgraphics/projects/narrator/780e0415-ca0e-4db6-986c-f3891ec9f11d.json"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://asset.wsj.net/wsjnewsgraphics/projects/narrator/780e0415-ca0e-4db6-986c-f3891ec9f11d.json  }}}\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14Republicans leading the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/republicans-narrow-control-of-house-sets-up-barnburner-in-2024-14d242d9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 party's effort to defend the House  }}}\uc2  in this fall's elections are pushing GOP colleagues to openly discuss {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/florida-abortion-rulings-to-keep-issue-front-and-center-in-november-1168511c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 their positions on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , rather than try to sidestep the issue like many did in the previous campaign, arguing that doing so will be critical to winning competitive races.\par \par \uc2 A memo prepared by House Republicans' campaign arm and viewed by The Wall Street Journal says Republicans have a "brand problem, not a policy problem," as their reluctance to discuss the issue left it to Democrats to define where the GOP stood. Many voters view the party's hopefuls as opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under any circumstances, when there are actually a variety of positions held by candidates, particularly in swing districts, the memo states.\par \par \uc2 The guidance tells candidates that they must "confidently articulate" their stance and that "being unwilling to stake out a clear position with voters is the worst possible solution."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weighed on the party's success in recent years. Republicans won back the House in 2022 but by a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/frustrated-republicans-try-to-explain-lack-of-midterm-red-wave-11668036382"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 narrower-than-expected margin  }}}\uc2 , a far cry from the "red wave" they had hoped would sweep them to a big majority in the chamber. Lawmakers from both parties say the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court's decision  }}}\uc2  that year ending the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  revved up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights voters and helped Democrats defend seats.\par \par \uc2 The party is trying to figure out a better approach headed into the 2024 contests, when the White House, the Senate and the House are all seen as tossups.\par \par \uc2 The guidance, which was presented to GOP colleagues by Rep. Richard Hudson (R., N.C.), chair of the National Republican Congressional Committee, at a three-day policy conference in West Virginia, also recommended that members express empathy for women, discuss "common sense" solutions and push back on what it calls Democrats' extremism on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Hudson backed up his argument with surveys conducted in more than 60 competitive House districts by Kellyanne Conway, a pollster and former senior adviser to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The polling found that about one-third of people surveyed associated Republicans with wanting to outlaw all abortions, while a similar number showed respondents saw Democrats favoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for any reason at any time. Republicans say that underneath the basic \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights and antiabortion labels, voters generally back some restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , putting them more in line with the GOP than Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Hudson, in remarks to reporters, listed immigration, crime and the economy as the issues that will draw voters to Republican candidates this fall.\par \par \uc2 "The issues align with Republicans," he said. "Americans are watching the sun set on the last four years, and they're ready for change."\par \par \uc2 Democrats counter that they are better aligned with voters. If Republicans "think people need to hear more about their unpopular antiabortion policies, go ahead. We're confident voters will respond accordingly in November," said Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee spokesman Viet Shelton.\par \par \uc2 Republicans' message has been a muddle for the past two years, with some members backing new federal restrictions and others saying the matter should be left to the states. More than a dozen GOP-controlled states have enacted a near ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many of those with few exceptions for rape or incest. Other state legislatures, like Florida, have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy, with few exceptions. Florida's law has since been tied up in court, so the state has a ban after 15 weeks, with few exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Trump, the Republicans' likely presidential nominee, hasn't been clear on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-takes-heat-from-religious-conservatives-for-softness-on-abortion-bans-c856397b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 where he stands  }}}\uc2 . The former president has taken credit for the ruling overturning Roe v. Wade\u8212\'20\'14he put three conservative justices on the high court during his administration\u8212\'20\'14while also criticizing Florida's law as a "terrible thing and a terrible mistake."\par \par \uc2 While some congressional Republicans do support near-complete bans on abortions or restrictions that kick in at six weeks, others have backed bans after 15 weeks of pregnancy. Some support exceptions for rape, incest or when life of the mother is at stake. The memo says Republican candidates should also push Democrats to state whether they would back any limitations on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have largely stayed away from setting a number of weeks in pregnancy at which the procedure should be banned, saying it should be between a woman and her doctor, not determined by the government. President Biden is campaigning on establishing nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, including through medicated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as with mifepristone, which can be used to end a pregnancy through 10 weeks of gestation.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How should Republicans talk about the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the 2024 campaign? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Issues around \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  are already causing headaches for Republicans in this election cycle. The party was forced to rush {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/alabama-shields-ivf-providers-with-new-law-53d54d16"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to defend in vitro fertilization  }}}\uc2  in February following a ruling by an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/major-alabama-health-system-pauses-ivf-treatments-after-state-court-says-frozen-embryos-qualify-as-children-71ed704e?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 all-Republican Alabama court  }}}\uc2  casting doubt on the legality of the procedure in the post-Roe world.\par \par \uc2 More than half of the GOP conference had signed on to a bill defining a "human being" as beginning at "the moment of fertilization, cloning, or other moment at which an individual member of the human species comes into being." That had raised questions about where the party stood on in vitro fertilization, used by many American couples who struggled to conceive children.\par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and IVF, the House Republican Conference chair, Rep. \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  of New York, said she thought "members have done a very strong job on messaging on" those issues. "We believe it's important for our members to engage on this and not stick their heads in the sand, which I think some potential candidates had done in the past," she told reporters at the West Virginia conference.\par \par \uc2 Rep. Michelle Steel of California was a vulnerable Republican who had signed on to the bill that said life begins at conception. She took her name off the bill this past week, saying on the House floor that she was removing herself "from the bill because it could create confusion about my support for the blessings of having children through IVF."\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , who represents a New York district that Biden won in 2020, on Wednesday became the first Republican to sign on to the Democrats' bill granting federal protections to women who use in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 "It's always good to know your district," said Oregon Rep. Lori Chavez-DeRemer, a Republican who represents a district that Biden won. She said her home state has laws protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, "so for me, this is something that Oregonians\u8230\'20\'26want access to, and they have it."\par \par \uc2 Both parties know it is a key issue for voters. In a Journal poll this past month, voters of both parties ranked \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the top issue that they must agree with a candidate's stance on. When asked to name the one issue on which they wouldn't vote for a candidate who disagreed with them, 22% said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The second-most-cited issue, at 20%, was immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 Katy Stech Ferek contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Natalie Andrews at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:natalie.andrews@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 natalie.andrews@wsj.com  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240313ek3d0015p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
