\par \par \uc2 An estimated 2% of births in the U.S. occur with the aid of fertility services. PHOTO: Kayana Szymczak for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion groups that are often courted by GOP candidates have said they expect lawsuits in other states that could lead to more protections for embryos. A plaintiff in Denton, Texas, is already suing, saying the state's new antiabortion law protects her embryos from in vitro fertilization as people, not property.\par \par \uc2 The fate of IVF in other states could hang in the balance because of the rise of fetal personhood laws and a push by antiabortion groups, now motivated by the Alabama court ruling, to bring the legal issue before more courts. Twenty-five bills have been introduced as of Monday to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by establishing fetal personhood, which confers legal rights at conception, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  , a policy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Fetal personhood laws have been used to incarcerate women in some states who were pregnant or gave birth and had a substance abuse history. In some states, such laws have let women sue for back child support since conception.\par \par \uc2 Some antiabortion groups such as SBA Pro-Life America want other states to adopt laws such as a Louisiana statute that prevents the intentional destruction of human embryos, including those not yet implanted in the womb.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Can the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights help propel the Democrats to victory in 2024? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 States have previously considered legislation that would have placed new requirements on in vitro fertilization. Lawmakers in South Dakota in 2021 considered a bill that would have required providers to inform the state about how many embryos were stored, created or implanted. A bill in Virginia's legislature in 2023 aimed to establish rights at conception with no exemption for embryos created by in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 Clarke Forsythe\uc2  , senior counsel at the antiabortion group \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  , said Alabama could potentially opt to limit in vitro fertilization to single-embryo transfers. Some countries have similar restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The public is generally supportive of in vitro fertilization treatment, polling has found.\par \par \uc2 "Unless your supporters are very heavily conservative, this is against the majority of Republicans," said Robert Blendon, an emeritus public health professor at \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  . "It's not great for Republicans."\par \par \uc2 Natalie Andrews contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Stephanie Armour at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Stephanie.Armour@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stephanie.Armour@wsj.com  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240228ek2s001bc\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
