\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court to Rule on Emergency Abortions; Justices will hear arguments on whether states can restrict Medicare-funded hospitals from providing an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  to stabilize a patient\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin and Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 556 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 January 2024\par 02:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Supreme Court said Friday it would hear Idaho's challenge to federal regulations requiring that Medicare-funded hospitals provide abortions when necessary to stabilize a patient during a medical emergency, and allowed the state to enforce its stricter rule allowing abortions only to prevent a woman's death.\par \par \uc2 Oral arguments in the case are expected in April, with a decision likely before July.\par \par \uc2 Lower courts have reached different conclusions on whether the federal Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, or EMTALA, pre-empts state restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In August, a federal district judge in Boise, Idaho, agreed with the Biden administration that in emergency situations the federal law took precedence over Idaho's Defense of Life Act, whose narrow exception allows the procedure to prevent the patient's death. On Tuesday, a federal appeals court in New Orleans found otherwise, holding that the federal law didn't exempt hospitals from Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 In its Supreme Court appeal, Idaho said that after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 justices overturned Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2  in 2022 and rescinded women's constitutional right to end unwanted pregnancies, the Biden administration "adopted the novel view that EMTALA creates a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in emergency rooms, even though EMTALA is silent on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and actually requires stabilizing treatment for the unborn children of pregnant women."\par \par \uc2 The state noted that the Idaho Supreme Court found that the Defense of Life Act's allowance for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a woman's death "leaves wide room for the physician's 'good faith medical judgment'" that the procedure is necessary.\par \par \uc2 In its opposition brief, the Justice Department said that exception is narrower than the federal law's requirements, which protect "patients not only from imminent death but also from emergencies that seriously threaten their health. Idaho law thus criminalizes care required by federal law" because under the state statute, "an emergency room physician who concludes that a pregnant woman needs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize a condition that would otherwise threaten serious and irreversible harm" can't perform the procedure "until the patient's condition deteriorates to the point where an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is needed to save her life."\par \par \uc2 In a statement, President Biden said the high court's order "allows Idaho's extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to go back into effect and denies women critical emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care required by federal law." He called on Congress to "immediately restore the protections of Roe v. Wade so that women in every state can access the healthcare they need."\par \par \uc2 Idaho's Republican attorney general, \uc2 Ra\u250\'00\'FAl Labrador\uc2  , praised the Supreme Court order. Federal and Idaho law "share the same goal: to save the lives of all women and their unborn children," he said. "The people of Idaho have spoken with clarity on the issue of life."\par \par \uc2 The emergency-treatment case is the second major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  matter on the court's docket this term. In December, the Supreme Court said it would review a challenge to federal regulations easing restrictions on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/health/supreme-court-to-decide-abortion-pill-mifepristone-regulation-challenge-4db2f6b2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pill mifepristone  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Write to Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Jess.Bravin@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jess.Bravin@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Laura Kusisto at \par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020240105ek15007pt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
