\par\par \uc2 Molly Ball\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 How  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 -Rights Backers Changed Their Message\u8212\'20\'14and Started Winning; Supporters of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access have emphasized 'freedom' and 'values' in successful campaigns in red-leaning states, with more to come in 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 By Molly Ball \par \uc2 1,791 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 December 2023\par 05:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Shortly after November's state-level elections affirmed voters' support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Virginia, Kentucky and Ohio, a Democratic pollster named Angela Kuefler got on a webinar to deliver an analysis\u8212\'20\'14and a warning\u8212\'20\'14to her fellow progressives. Yes, it was clear \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a winning issue, she said, but it mattered a lot how advocates talked about it.\par \par \uc2 "Talking about this in the context of values really widens our support," said Kuefler, an adviser to the Nov. 7 ballot initiative in Ohio that added a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the state's constitution, winning by nearly 14 points in a state President Biden lost by eight. By values, she explained, she was principally talking about the idea of freedom. In polling by Kuefler's firm, Global Strategy Group, majorities answered "yes" to both "Should we restore the rights we had under Roe v. Wade?" and "Should personal decisions like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  be up to women rather than the government?" But the latter statement outperformed the former by a whopping 19-point margin, she noted, adding, "It's the values language that allows us to win by such big margins."\par \par \uc2 Since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court overturned Roe }}}\uc2  18 months ago in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, voters have taken the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights side in seven straight statewide ballots from Kansas to Montana to Michigan. Support for what was long seen as a divisive issue has turned lopsided: In the latest Wall Street Journal poll, 62% said the procedure should be mostly or completely legal, a seven-point increase from before the court's action. Such shifts on long-debated issues are rare and potentially seismic in an electorate closely divided along partisan lines.\par \par \uc2 It was the Dobbs decision itself that most powerfully changed the discussion, spurring widespread anger by revoking a right many had taken for granted and giving rise to tragic stories in states where the procedure has been outlawed, from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/indiana-abortion-doctor-fined-for-violating-privacy-of-10-year-old-patient-94e423f5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 10-year-old Ohio rape victim }}}\uc2  who had to leave her state to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/texas-woman-who-sought-exception-to-abortion-ban-decides-to-leave-state-e99f16f5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas mother }}}\uc2  who was blocked by her state's supreme court from aborting a fetus with a fatal genetic condition. But many advocates believe the shift has also been driven by a subtle but powerful change in messaging that has reshaped the way liberals talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fundamentally changing the terms of the highly charged debate and leaving conservatives scrambling to respond.\par \par \uc2 Going forward, many Democrats see the issue's success\u8212\'20\'14at a time when their party's stances on many other issues are unpopular\u8212\'20\'14as a crucial political asset: not only as a way to drive turnout in the 2024 presidential election but also a road map for appealing to voters' fundamental values on issues from the economy to education.\par \par \uc2 The change is evident to close observers of political discourse. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists rarely use the term "pro-choice" anymore, preferring to talk about people's "freedom to decide." In September, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights group Naral Pro-Choice America, founded in 1969, changed its name to \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2 . Grounded in research that predates the Dobbs ruling, these new buzzwords have helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights side resonate across partisan lines. Republicans have noticed the resonance with their liberty-loving voters. "They stole freedom!" one antiabortion Republican consultant recently remarked.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/kansas-abortion-vote-results-11659440554"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kansas' ballot campaign }}}\uc2  in 2022 was backed by a group called Kansans for Constitutional Freedom, while a group working to put the issue on the ballot in Arkansas is called Arkansans for Limited Government. Ads for the Ohio referendum emphasized that it was about "ensuring families\u8212\'20\'14not government\u8212\'20\'14have the freedom to make their own personal decisions."\par \par \uc2 Successful Democratic candidates in the 2022 midterms drew on the same themes, as when Arizona Sen. Mark Kelly used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a springboard to blast his Republican opponent, Blake Masters, as "someone who thinks he knows better than everyone about everything" in a debate. Kentucky Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , a Democrat, won a surprisingly wide five-point victory in his red state with help from a viral ad featuring a young woman talking about becoming pregnant at age 12 after being raped by her stepfather.\par \par \uc2 Contrary to popular belief, it wasn't suburban women who reacted most strongly to the ad, said Beshear's campaign manager, Eric Hyers. "The voters that moved the most were older, rural, conservative men who were registered Republicans," Hyers said. "Those are the voters who have never been forced to think about 'What if a 12-year-old gets raped by her stepfather? What then?' It's not about labels, pro-this, pro-that. It's about what you think should happen to the little girl in that situation."\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion advocates have struggled to respond. Beshear's Republican opponent, Daniel Cameron, changed his position late in the campaign to favor exceptions. Ads against the Ohio ballot initiative painted it as going too far or pointed to alleged consequences unrelated to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Virginia, Republican legislative candidates tried to sell voters on a "moderate" limit of 15 weeks. None of these approaches have worked.\par \par \uc2 Some antiabortion advocates believe Democrats have overplayed their hand on the issue. "Republicans look like they're on defense here, but they have no reason to be," said \uc2 Ralph Reed\uc2 , the veteran Christian conservative and chairman of the Faith and Freedom Coalition. He called for the party to "get back on offense and portray Democrats as the extremists they are. You can't surrender the ultimate objective, protection for every unborn life."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  chairwoman, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , has urged her party to confront the issue. A party memo from 2022 counseled candidates to "stake out common ground with the majority of Americans who support exceptions" to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions for rape, incest and the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 The party's likely presidential nominee, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , is running campaign ads in Iowa boasting of "living up to his promise, Roe v. Wade was overturned"\u8212\'20\'14even as the former president has fretted about the political fallout from Dobbs. Many Republicans privately see the issue as a loser but fear their base won't allow them to moderate their positions.\par \par \uc2 For \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists, the political triumph is bittersweet; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 14 states have completely banned \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  since Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 "We had a theory that has proven correct, though 'I told you so' doesn't feel good when you've lost everything," said Ilyse Hogue, who served as Naral's president until 2021. The theory was that despite polls showing a rigid "pro-choice/pro-life" binary, "under the right conditions, supporting the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a majority position"\u8212\'20\'14but that liberals weren't tapping into the deep-seated emotional triggers that would push people in that direction.\par \par \uc2 Starting in 2014, when the Supreme Court partially struck down the Affordable Care Act's contraceptive mandate, Hogue commissioned research to better capture people's sentiments beyond simply asking whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should or shouldn't be legal. Working with linguists and psychologists, the researchers convened Americans in open-ended focus groups aimed at understanding their emotional commitments. They found that most people saw the issue in shades of gray, and recoiled from political language that didn't reflect that complexity.\par \par \uc2 "The messaging we were using wasn't working, and we knew we had to get at deeper emotions, versus what people say they think," said Adrienne Kimmell, who spearheaded the project as Naral's chief of research at the time. A plurality of voters were conflicted: They said they wouldn't seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  themselves but didn't think the government should prevent others from doing so. Focusing on those voters, the researchers found that the idea of freedom was central to their self-image as Americans, an idea that resonated with men and women alike and across ages, races and geographies.\par \par \uc2 The attachment to freedom was accompanied by a deep hostility to its opposite\u8212\'20\'14control\u8212\'20\'14and a feeling that such choices were personal and shouldn't be subject to politics. In a 2019 focus group in Arizona, a Latino man said, "We live in the United States and we can make those decisions because America is for freedom and justice."\par \par \uc2 Many Democratic politicians had preferred to talk about "reproductive healthcare" out of concern that "\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 " was seen as indelicate. Biden, a Catholic who once described himself as pro-life but has moved left on the issue, is among the Democrats who have seemed uneasy with the word. But the research found that voters disliked evasive language, and didn't find the word "\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 " off-putting. In keeping with this finding, the Ohio initiative's signs said "Vote yes, protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights."\par \par \uc2 "I support the freedom to decide" might seem like a straight-up synonym for "I'm pro-choice," but the research found major differences in the effectiveness of the two sentences. "Choice" had become a political buzzword, linked to the bloodless abstraction of "rights," and a label that many didn't see as part of their identity. A similar linguistic and conceptual shift powered the public's increasing support of same-sex marriage as advocates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2012/12/the-marriage-plot-inside-this-years-epic-campaign-for-gay-equality/265865/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 switched from talking about "gay rights" }}}\uc2  to talking about the universal values of love and commitment and the "freedom to marry" whomever one chooses.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Will Democrats' message on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lead to victories for their party in 2024? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 The progressives' research does suggest limits to this approach. In the abstract, many voters support the idea of limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially for minors, even if they see them as problematic in practice. Kuefler's polling found voters are nearly as likely to see Democrats as extreme on the issue as they are Republicans. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups have also vastly outspent their opposition in ballot campaigns to date: The Ohio referendum backers spent more than $30 million, nearly three times as much as the measure's opponents.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, Kuefler said, must not assume that simply {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/abortion-rights-election-ballot-2024-3c50731d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 putting \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  on the ballot }}}\uc2  will automatically be a winner as activists work to do so in multiple states in 2024. But they can seize on the success of the "freedom" message and tie it to other issues, such as Republicans' attempts to limit books in school libraries or gender-reassignment treatments, or even conservative policies on healthcare, taxes and crime that liberals argue circumscribe people's autonomy.\par \par \uc2 "The 'freedom' argument both speaks to a value we have and undercuts a Republican brand advantage," Kuefler said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Molly Ball at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:molly.ball@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 molly.ball@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231230ejcu0008h\par }\page {\par