\par\par \uc2 Essay\par \uc2 Life\par \b \uc2 The Trump Revolution Among U.S. Evangelicals; As evangelicalism becomes a 'bottom-up' religion, congregations are pressuring their pastors to offer a more hard-edged political message.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Francis X. Rocca and Kris Maher \par \uc2 1,382 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 December 2023\par 17:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 No political issue over the last half-century has mobilized evangelical Christian voters more effectively than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Yet going into next month's Republican presidential caucuses in Iowa, where evangelicals are a strong presence, the candidates with the strongest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stances are lagging in the polls. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who called Florida's ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks "a terrible mistake," retains a large lead.\par \par \uc2 Support for Trump despite his hedging on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be largely a question of gratitude, since he named three \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justices who voted to overturn a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it also suggests that many evangelicals now support Trump for reasons other than that longstanding priority.\par \par \uc2 Evangelical Christianity is undergoing a populist revolution. Long in the making but catalyzed by the rise of Trump, this shift has inspired many evangelicals to reject their own elites and take a more militant posture against perceived enemies. At a growing number of churches, evangelicals hear sermons on a range of controversial political subjects, from border security to gun rights.\par \par \uc2 Preaching to a flock of several dozen people this summer in eastern Tennessee, pastor Shahram Hadian denounced vaccine mandates, voiced doubts about the results of the 2020 presidential race and said that Trump's poll numbers were rising, despite multiple indictments, because a remnant of people still loyal to God were finally waking up.\par \par \uc2 "Here we are ramping up for 2024 and another crucial election, if they don't steal it or try to indict their way out of it," Hadian said. "Our response must be, we will not comply. Amen!" An increasing number of people who support such positions now call themselves evangelicals even though they aren't members of any church, or attend rarely.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What do you think explains \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's appeal to evangelicals? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Trump "detected and tapped into an enormous anxiety, quite legitimate anxiety, about the culture and quite understandable frustration in the political process," said R. Albert Mohler Jr., president of the Southern Baptist Theological Seminary. "The immediate attraction is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying, 'All these other guys have sold you out, all of them. I'm not going to sell you out.'"\par \par \uc2 The term "evangelical Christian" traditionally refers to a form of Protestantism emphasizing certain theological principles, including the inerrancy of the Bible and the experience of personal conversion or being "born again" in Christ. Twenty-four percent of U.S. adults described themselves as born-again or evangelical Protestants in 2021, according to the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 , and the nation's largest Protestant denomination, the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 , is considered an evangelical church.\par \par \uc2 Evangelical involvement with politics didn't begin with the 2016 election. \uc2 Billy Graham\uc2  advised presidents of both parties, from \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  to \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . In the 1970s, \uc2 Jerry Falwell\uc2  founded the Moral Majority to promote conservative social policies, including opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to gay teachers in public schools. President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  courted evangelical support, as did President George H.W. Bush. Evangelicals reached the peak of their political prominence when one of their own, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , won the White House on a platform of "compassionate conservatism."\par \par \uc2 It was a tribute to evangelical prestige that Obama invited the Southern Baptist pastor Rick Warren to pray at his first inauguration, in 2009, despite the two men's differences on the key political and cultural issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 During the Obama administration, however, conservative Christians experienced a setback to their agenda after Vice President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and then Obama himself backed same-sex marriage, followed by the Supreme Court's recognition of the practice in 2015. Moreover, after decades of supporting candidates who campaigned on their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , access to the procedure remained a constitutional right. Frustrated evangelicals responded to Trump's promises to defend them, said Philip Gorski, a professor of sociology at \uc2 Yale University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some prominent evangelicals deplore their community's support for Trump, notwithstanding the Supreme Court victory on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Voting for someone who is an admitted sexual abuser in order to hopefully see pro-life laws put in place is a Faustian bargain," said Karen Swallow Prior, a literary critic and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist, referring to the 2005 "Access Hollywood" recording in which Trump used vulgar language to describe sexually assaulting women.\par \par \uc2 Timon Cline, editor in chief of the American Reformer magazine, a publication associated with a movement called the New Christian Right, said Trump set an example for evangelicals with his defiance of elites. "It was cathartic to see someone at least be disruptive of the status quo," he said.\par \par \uc2 Cline's magazine has followed that example with an activist arm that avowedly aims to "reform and revitalize" church-associated institutions. He says they campaigned for the \uc2 SBC\uc2  to affirm its prohibition of women serving as pastors at the denomination's annual assembly in June. That position prevailed overwhelmingly despite the appeals of Rick Warren, who remains one of the country's most famous evangelicals but whose Saddleback Church has been expelled by the \uc2 SBC\uc2  over the issue.\par \par \uc2 "Evangelicalism has become a bottom-up religion, more than a top-down religion," said Ryan Burge, a professor of political science at \uc2 Eastern Illinois University\uc2 . "There is no one who speaks for evangelicalism like there used to be. There's no \uc2 Billy Graham\uc2 , there's no Falwell, there's no [Pat] Robertson." He noted that \uc2 Franklin Graham\uc2 , the son of \uc2 Billy Graham\uc2 , was excoriated on social media when he encouraged people to get vaccinated against Covid. "His dad was the pope of modern evangelicalism. And he's super conservative, and he loves Trump," Burge said. "Even he can't guide the evangelical flock anymore."\par \par \uc2 "I do not speak for all evangelicals, and my father \uc2 Billy Graham\uc2  would have said the same thing," said \uc2 Franklin Graham\uc2  in response to a request for comment. "While evangelicals may not completely agree on every social, political, or cultural issue, we unite around what matters most\u8212\'20\'14the authority of God's Word and sharing the hope of Jesus Christ."\par \par \uc2 According to Kristin Du Mez, a professor of history at Calvin University, "this is a populist moment, and so it's hard for anybody to lead evangelicalism today\u8230\'20\'26What we see happening is leaders trying to run out in front of this movement."\par \par \uc2 Pastors are under pressure to take a more polemical line in preaching, said \uc2 Russell Moore\uc2 , a former official at the \uc2 SBC\uc2 . He left the denomination over disagreements that included friction with other leaders over his criticism of Trump, and has written a critique of contemporary evangelicalism entitled "Losing Our Religion."\par \par \uc2 "It's not enough that one's church didn't stay closed during the pandemic; it was also that the pastor didn't castigate the churches who did or talk about the government conspiracy to shut down the churches," Moore said. "Most congregations have a faction that is like this, and the congregations who don't are holding their breaths waiting for one."\par \par \uc2 Mohler, the \uc2 SBC\uc2  seminary president, speaks of "MAGA churches," where "the Gospel has been eclipsed by political messaging." He says he can foresee such churches developing into a "post-Christian, explicitly nihilistic" ideology like the far right in some European countries.\par \par \uc2 "I'd be very interested to find out how many of the MAGA folks really are committed to stopping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mohler said, stressing that evangelical voters must press Trump to clarify his stand on the issue.\par \par \uc2 One of Trump's most ardent evangelical supporters, Robert James Jeffress Jr., pastor of First Baptist Church in Dallas, says that the former president is still committed to opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that his recent comments merely reflect a realistic legislative strategy to that end.\par \par \uc2 But Hadian, the pastor in Tennessee, says the matter calls for vigilance. "We need to pray for him, because I see he's a different man than he was in 2016 when he was the outsider," Hadian said recently to followers online. "We've got to be careful not to put all our hope and trust in one man."\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231221ejcl003e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
