\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills, Fishing Boats and the Legal War on Experts; Cases before the Supreme Court could endanger regulation of banking and many other sectors.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan S. Blinder \par \uc2 786 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 December 2023\par 22:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Pop quiz: What do \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, observers on fishing boats and minimum capital requirements for banks have in common?\par \par \uc2 If you answered "nothing," you get half-credit. Substantively, the three have nothing to do with one another. Banks don't operate on fishing vessels or dispense abortifacients. You get only half-credit, however, because there is a real danger the Supreme Court will knit them together in 2024.\par \par \uc2 You may have read that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes won a lower court ruling in April that would stop mail-order delivery of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone. (The high court stayed that order, pending further review.) This is no small matter. Mifepristone, used with another drug, misoprostol, is the most common method of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the U.S. The Food and Drug Administration approved it as safe and effective in 2000 and later eased regulations on how it is dispensed.\par \par \uc2 Therein lies the nub of the legal case, \uc2 FDA\uc2  v. Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, which the Supreme Court added to its docket for this term. The central issue is whether plaintiffs can use courtrooms and judges to second-guess the scientific decisions of \uc2 FDA\uc2  experts. The agency, the Biden administration, and mifepristone's manufacturers argue the answer should be no.\par \par \uc2 If the justices answer yes, the precedent they create won't end with mifepristone. It probably won't even end with \uc2 FDA\uc2  drug approvals. Rather, it could threaten one of the foundations of the administrative state: that regulators may make decisions when Congress has granted them the authority to do so.\par \par \uc2 That's where the fishing vessels come in. In Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo, four family-owned and -operated companies are suing the Commerce Department's National Marine Fisheries Service, which wants some fishing-boat operators to pay the costs of federal observers who are on board to watch for overfishing. In Loper, the lower courts deferred to the agency's regulatory decision, which is the norm under the so-called \uc2 Chevron\uc2  doctrine. The company has appealed, and the case is scheduled for oral argument on Jan. 17. The rest of us should care, because the Commerce Department and the \uc2 Chevron\uc2  doctrine are both on trial.\par \par \uc2 In \uc2 Chevron\uc2  v. \uc2 Natural Resources Defense Council\uc2  (1984), the court ruled that federal judges should defer to administrative agencies when interpreting ambiguous statutes that fall within their purview. Ironically, conservatives loved the decision at the time because environmentalist plaintiffs lost to President Reagan's Environmental Protection Agency.\par \par \uc2 Lately, however, the script has flipped and \uc2 Chevron\uc2  has become a b\u234\'00\'EAte noire of the right. Opposing \uc2 Chevron\uc2  in lower-court decisions was one of Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 's springboards to national prominence. His conservative colleagues on the court also seem generally hostile to the doctrine. If they overturn it in Loper, America's administrative law will be left in shambles. Who will decide on the detailed regulations needed to carry out laws Congress passes? Judges without relevant expertise?\par \par \uc2 That's why a onetime bank regulator like me brought up capital requirements. You may have seen one of the commercials now running on TV claiming the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is about to thrust a dagger into the heart of American capitalism. The issue is how much capital banks should be required to hold as safety cushions against large losses. Remember \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 ? The Fed and other banking agencies think banks need more, and they are considering using their regulatory authority to make it so. The banks are less than delighted.\par \par \uc2 The optimal amount of bank capital is a complicated issue with reasonable arguments on both sides. I won't attempt to resolve the debate. More important is that if the plaintiffs win in the mifepristone and fishing-boat cases, the banking agencies' authority to set minimum capital requirements could be further undermined.\par \par \uc2 Nor will the evisceration of the administrative state end there. U.S. law is crammed with financial regulations handed down by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , \uc2 Federal Deposit Insurance Corp.\uc2 , \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  and other agencies over the years. All need interpretations. If the government loses in both cases, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and Commerce Department won't be the only losers. Judges could call into question the financial, health, safety and commercial regulations across the federal bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 The justices doubtless know that. Their rulings ought to show a little judicial restraint. Will they?\par \par \uc2 Mr. Blinder is a professor of economics and public affairs at Princeton and a visiting fellow at the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2 . He served as vice chairman of the Federal Reserve, 1994-96.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231220ejck006mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
