\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 's Long-Shot Hopes Rest on New Hampshire; Former New Jersey governor is banking on state's open primary system to blunt Trump's rise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eliza Collins | Photographs by Sophie Park for The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 1,213 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2023\par 14:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDONDERRY, N.H.\u8212\'20\'14Chris Christie's most promising path to the Republican presidential nomination is in winning over voters who aren't Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Christie, a moderate former New Jersey governor, has centered his long-shot bid in New Hampshire because the state's open primary system allows independents to cast ballots in either party's primary. The state's Jan. 23 primary\u8212\'20\'14the second contest in the GOP nominating process\u8212\'20\'14provides Christie's best hope of reversing former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's march toward his third nomination.\par \par \uc2 Trump leads all early state and national polls by wide margins. In New Hampshire, Christie lags behind not only Trump but also former United Nations Ambassador and South Carolina Gov. Nikki Haley, although Christie and Haley are separated only by single digits, according to recent polls.\par \par \uc2 In other early states, such as Iowa, Christie is polling behind several other candidates. A recent Wall Street Journal national poll had Trump with 59% of the vote, Haley with 15%, Florida Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  with 14%, entrepreneur \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  at 4% and Christie at 2%.\par \par \uc2 Yet, Christie is plowing forward with an unorthodox campaign by attempting to capture voters outside the Republican base. He is attacking Trump, with whom he was once close, calling him a liar and saying he is unfit to be president. He opened a town hall this week criticizing some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as too aggressive and saying he would not sign a federal ban if he is elected. Even on issues such as immigration, where he is in step with the GOP base on border security, he has said he would find a compromise with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Thalia Floras, 61 years old, a Nashua, N.H., resident who works in retail, said Christie is the GOP candidate most aligned with her on the issues. That's because Floras is a lifelong Democrat who switched her party identification to independent to be able to vote in the Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 Floras is deciding between Christie and Haley, and said she would vote for the one with the best chance of blunting Trump's candidacy. Given Haley's recent momentum, including an endorsement from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/new-hampshire-gov-chris-sununu-set-to-endorse-nikki-haley-in-gop-presidential-race-97545648"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state's popular Republican governor }}}\uc2 , \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , this week, Floras feels she will end up casting her ballot for Haley.\par \par \uc2 "My heart is with Christie, but my brain is with Haley," she said.\par \par \uc2 Supporters at events this week for both Christie and Haley\u8212\'20\'14many of them independents or even Democrats\u8212\'20\'14said they were ready to move on from Trump. They were torn between which of the two candidates could be most competitive.\par \par \uc2 Christie has begun criticizing Haley more aggressively on the campaign trail. During his opening remarks on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he went after the only woman in the race for what he said was trying to make everyone happy. Haley, who told Iowa voters she would have signed a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if she was South Carolina governor, also has said the party should stop talking about a national ban because it is unlikely to happen with a divided Congress.\par \par \uc2 Election results and polls across the country show that access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is popular, including in New Hampshire, where voters of all ideologies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/new-hampshire-tests-abortions-role-in-midterm-elections-11654347601"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 support access to the procedure }}}\uc2 . However, the issue is divisive with Republican voters, particularly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/gop-presidential-hopefuls-aim-to-gain-on-trump-by-wooing-evangelicals-2ea3187d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 evangelical voters }}}\uc2  who make up a significant part of the GOP electorate in Iowa and South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 "On Gov. Haley's part, if the question is really hard, she wants to make everybody happy," Christie said during his town hall. "Beware of the person who won't answer your question." He has also continued to question why Haley and others in the race aren't more critical of Trump.\par \par \uc2 The Haley campaign didn't respond to a request for comment. During a town hall in New Hampshire this week, Haley was asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She replied that she is "unapologetically pro-life" but called for consensus on the issue. When asked about her position on Trump she has said: "Anti-Trumpers don't think I hate Trump enough. Pro-Trumpers don't think I love Trump enough."\par \par \uc2 Christie's approach has made an impression on Gary Goudreau, 66, a retiree from Chester, N.H., and an independent voter who will support Christie in the GOP primary. "He's not afraid to say his positions and the others won't," he said.\par \par \uc2 Former U.S. Rep. Charles Bass (R., N.H.), who has endorsed Christie, said the candidate struck him as honest, and he admired that: "Perhaps not the best path to victory, but certainly the way Americans should be treated by their elected officials," Bass said.\par \par \uc2 Christie, a charismatic two-term governor and former U.S. attorney, does well with New Hampshire's intimate campaign settings. He holds lengthy question-and-answer sessions, frequently cracking jokes and doing impressions.\par \par \uc2 He told the Wall Street Journal he expects independent voters to make up close to half of the primary electorate in New Hampshire: "They better be a big part of everybody's strategy who wants to win," he said.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear, however, how many independent voters will cast ballots in the GOP primary. Undeclared voters, or independents, are the state's largest voting bloc. More of them might participate in the GOP race because the state's Democratic primary won't count on the path to that party's nomination because of a dispute with the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  campaign over the scheduling of state nominating contests. In 2016, the last time Republicans had a competitive primary in New Hampshire, a CNN exit poll had independent voters making up 42% of the GOP primary electorate. Trump won New Hampshire that year.\par \par \uc2 Christie has said he doesn't need to come in first in New Hampshire but will need to show that he can be competitive. If Trump gets under 50%, the former president will be wounded, Christie said. Christie's plan is then to remain in the race as other candidates drop out and focus on winning other states where not just Republicans vote in the primaries, such as Michigan. That is a difficult path because Trump could be racking up delegates throughout the process and secure the nomination as early as March.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Should \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  stay in the race? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Christie says he is the only candidate trying to beat Trump by confronting the former president head-on. But the longer the field remains crowded and vote shares remain divided, the harder it will be for any candidate to present a viable alternative to Trump.\par \par \uc2 Sununu, the New Hampshire governor who endorsed Haley, said the field should consolidate behind Haley.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not going anywhere," Christie told reporters when asked about Sununu's comments.\par \par \uc2 Barb Matto, 58, an airport employee from Derry, N.H., who is a Trump supporter, said there was no point in talking about Christie or any other candidate: "Nothing matters, Trump is gonna win."\par \par \uc2 Write to Eliza Collins at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:eliza.collins@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliza.collins@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231216ejcg000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
