\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills require a prescription from a healthcare provider. PHOTO: Michael Noble Jr. for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 How is medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  different from the morning-after pill?\par \par \uc2 While medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  terminates pregnancy, the morning-after pill, or emergency contraception, helps to prevent pregnancy by suppressing ovulation and curbing implantation. There are three types of emergency contraception pills: progestin-only pills such as levonorgestrel (sold under the brand name Plan B, among others), ulipristal (brand named Ella) and combined emergency contraception pills that consist of ethinyl estradiol and levonorgestrel.\par \par \uc2 Are \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills safe and effective?\par \par \uc2 Yes. Studies have shown the two-medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen to be safer than widely used drugs including acetaminophen, penicillin and Viagra. Less than 0.4% of patients using mifepristone and misoprostol to terminate their pregnancies were hospitalized or required blood transfusions, according to a 2013 scientific review of the efficacy and safety of the two-medication regimen.\par \par \uc2 Research has shown the misoprostol-only regimen to be similarly safe.\par \par \uc2 Common side effects of medication abortions include bleeding, cramping, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, headache and dizziness. The symptoms are typically short-lived and usually abate after a day or two.\par \par \uc2 The additional risk to patients taking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at home is minimal, so long as the medication is acquired from reliable sources, said Nisha Verma, an obstetrician-gynecologist and \uc2 ACOG\uc2  fellow.\par \par \uc2 "However, it is important that people taking medications at home have access to safe, nonjudgmental, and supportive care within the formal medical system if they need it at any point," Dr. Verma said. "People who do have questions or concerns before, during or after a self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be able to safely and confidently get the care that they need."\par \par \uc2 Following a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , patients experiencing prolonged fever and severe cramping\u8212\'20\'14that doesn't resolve with over-the-counter painkillers\u8212\'20\'14or have continued symptoms of pregnancy should seek medical care.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich427\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6405
