\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas Woman Who Sought Exception to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban Decides to Leave State; Hours later, the Texas Supreme Court ruled against woman with fatal fetal abnormality who sought in-state procedure\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 659 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2023\par 02:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A pregnant Texas woman locked in a legal battle over aborting a fetus with a fatal abnormality decided to travel elsewhere for the procedure, a move announced hours before the Texas Supreme Court ruled against her bid for an in-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The case appeared to be the first since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the overturning of Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  to test whether a pregnant litigant could use the courts to secure an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a state that outlawed the procedure with only an exception for certain medical emergencies.\par \par \uc2 Kate Cox, 31 years old, a mother of two who was 20 weeks pregnant when she filed the case, argued that she should qualify under medical exceptions to the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws because continuing the pregnancy would affect her health and fertility.\par \par \uc2 "This past week of legal limbo has been hellish for Kate," said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president and CEO at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Her health is on the line. She's been in and out of the emergency room and she couldn't wait any longer."\par \par \uc2 A trial judge last week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/texas-judge-grants-pregnant-womans-request-for-abortion-3d1af51f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued an order }}}\uc2  allowing Cox to obtain the procedure but the state Supreme Court on Friday put a temporary hold on that ruling. Then late Monday, the high court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.txcourts.gov/media/1457645/230994pc.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled }}}\uc2  Cox's doctor, Damla Karsan, hadn't adequately attested that Cox's condition met the state's exception.\par \par \uc2 "Dr. Karsan did not assert that Ms. Cox has a 'life-threatening physical condition' or that, in Dr. Karsan's reasonable medical judgment, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary because Ms. Cox has the type of condition the exception requires," the court wrote.\par \par \uc2 The seven-page opinion, however, sought to minimize the scope of the court's holding.\par \par \uc2 "A pregnant woman does not need a court order to have a lifesaving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas," the court wrote. "Our ruling today does not block a life-saving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this very case if a physician determines that one is needed under the appropriate legal standard, using reasonable medical judgment."\par \par \uc2 The office of Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment. Shortly after the trial court issued its ruling, Paxton, a Republican, sent a letter to the hospitals where Karsan practices saying the decision wouldn't insulate the doctor or hospitals from long-term civil or criminal liability if the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were performed.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  eliminated the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June 2022, pregnant women have been much more likely to leave states in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned to get the procedure elsewhere than remain and fight a legal battle that can take multiple days or weeks. \uc2 Cox\uc2 's legal roller coaster highlighted the hurdles to bringing such a challenge.\par \par \uc2 "Kate's case is proof that exceptions don't work, and it's dangerous to be pregnant in any state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban," said \uc2 Molly Duane\uc2 , one of \uc2 Cox\uc2 's lawyers.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/idaho-must-allow-abortions-in-certain-emergencies-judge-rules-11661386919"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have brought cases in a number of states }}}\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, including Idaho, North Dakota and Oklahoma, arguing that exceptions for medical emergencies are too narrow or too difficult for doctors to interpret. A separate case is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-judge-blocks-state-abortion-ban-against-women-with-health-risks-d139a7c0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pending before the Texas Supreme Court }}}\uc2  involving women who said they were unable to obtain abortions in Texas when they or their fetuses suffered life-threatening complications.\par \par \uc2 Cox\uc2 's case was unique because her continuing pregnancy added a sense of urgency to what has been more of an abstract question in other litigation. Following \uc2 Cox\uc2 's suit, an anonymous Kentucky woman, eight weeks pregnant, filed a case seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the basis that the state's bans violate rights to privacy and self-determination protected in Kentucky's constitution.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231211ejcb004v1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
