\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Arizona Is the Next  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Battleground; The state Supreme Court is set to hear arguments on whether to allow a near-total ban to take effect\u8212\'20\'14kicking off a 2024 showdown\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eliza Collins and Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,167 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2023\par 14:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 PHOENIX\u8212\'20\'14Arizona is poised to take center stage in state-level battles over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week when its highest court weighs whether to allow a near-total ban on the procedure to take effect.\par \par \uc2 The case will kick off an election-year showdown over \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is expected to heavily influence the outcomes of Arizona's hypercompetitive 2024 races, including contests for Congress and the White House. Activists are gearing up to put an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights measure on the ballot in Arizona next year.\par \par \uc2 The state Supreme Court is set to hear arguments on Tuesday about whether to allow a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/arizona-appeals-court-blocks-territorial-era-abortion-law-11665194904"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 near-total \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban }}}\uc2  dating back to 1864 to take effect. An appellate court a year ago ruled that the 19th-century measure needed to be harmonized with a more recent law banning most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, in effect allowing women in the state to have abortions up until that later point.\par \par \uc2 If the justices allow the older law to take effect for the first time in some 50 years, it could inflame tensions in the state and boost the ballot measure's chances of passage. Polling nationwide shows that most Americans want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legal either in all or most cases with some restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Voters have favored \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/abortion-rights-election-ballot-2024-3c50731d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seven consecutive state referendums }}}\uc2  since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade in June 2022. Since then, strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws have taken effect in some 16 states. The issue is also coming to the fore in Texas, where there is a legal fight over a judge's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/texas-judge-grants-pregnant-womans-request-for-abortion-3d1af51f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision to grant an emergency request }}}\uc2  allowing a pregnant woman whose fetus has a fatal diagnosis to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  there.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups are eyeing 2024 ballot referendums in at least half a dozen states, including Nevada and Florida, but they see Arizona as the most promising because it has a relatively straightforward process for getting a measure on the ballot, Democratic allies in influential offices, and voters sympathetic to messages about personal freedom.\par \par \uc2 The state will be a focus for both parties next year, with national money pouring in to influence the presidential race all the way down to the state legislature. Democrats see the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiative as a possible turnout boost in a state that was once a Republican bastion.\par \par \uc2 "We're a pretty independent-minded state in Arizona. For a lot of Republican women and independents, the Dobbs decision was a bridge too far," Arizona Attorney General Kris Mayes, a Democrat, said of the Supreme Court decision that overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 Mayes said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was central to her narrow {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/arizona-attorney-general-recount-shows-narrow-victory-for-democrat-kris-mayes-11672345845"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 280-vote victory }}}\uc2  in November 2022. Arizona Gov. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , another Democrat, made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access central to the campaign {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrat-katie-hobbs-defeats-kari-lake-in-high-profile-arizona-governor-race-11668479658"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she also won narrowly }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion activists are emphasizing that the ballot measure would protect abortions until viability and potentially open the door for abortions even later than that if a mother's life or health is in danger. Their campaign is called "It Goes Too Far."\par \par \uc2 "Just because someone says they are pro-choice that does not mean that they want [no restrictions]," said George Khalaf, a Republican strategist and senior adviser to the opposition effort.\par \par \uc2 In last year's midterm elections, Democrats in Arizona and other parts of the country won some major competitive statewide races by pulling in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/why-independent-voters-broke-for-democrats-in-the-midterms-11668249002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 independent and centrist Republican voters }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Two-thirds of Arizona voters said the Supreme Court's decision to eliminate a federal constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a factor in their 2022 vote, according to the election survey AP VoteCast. And 61% of those surveyed said they favored guaranteeing access to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Nowhere will the theory be tested more than in Arizona's first congressional district, which includes part of Phoenix and its suburbs. The district is represented by Rep. \uc2 David Schweikert\uc2  (R., Ariz.) but has otherwise trended away from the GOP in recent years as voters in this wealthy, highly educated area have been turned off by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies. President Biden narrowly won the district in 2020, but voters re-elected Schweikert that year and again in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Schweikert is seen as one of the most vulnerable Republicans in the country in part because of his antiabortion stance. Schweikert co-sponsored a bill before Roe was overturned that in effect would have banned abortions nationwide. In an interview, he said he would continue to vote antiabortion in Congress but is wrestling with what it means to support states' rights even when he doesn't like how the state votes.\par \par \uc2 He is not the only one. Republicans have struggled with how to campaign on antiabortion policies. Trump, the likely GOP nominee for president in 2024, touts his role in nominating several justices who voted to overturn Roe but has also said that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/trump-takes-heat-from-religious-conservatives-for-softness-on-abortion-bans-c856397b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 taking extreme stances on the issue }}}\uc2  has cost Republicans electorally.\par \par \uc2 At the Senate level, Kari Lake, who is expected to be the GOP nominee in the race for Independent \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 's Senate seat, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/kari-lake-to-announce-senate-run-setting-up-high-stakes-arizona-race-30ff1594"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told The Wall Street Journal }}}\uc2  she supports Arizona's current 15-week ban but would oppose a federal ban. However, she has previously taken a more restrictive stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that included praising the near-total ban.\par \par \uc2 A coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups is working to gather double the roughly 384,000 signatures required to put a constitutional amendment on Arizona's ballot to bolster the chance of making it through potential legal challenges. Antiabortion groups are seeking to make it harder to amend the constitution in some states.\par \par \uc2 Interviews in the district show that having a separate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure could give voters the opportunity to split their tickets and vote for the ballot measure as well as for candidates who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. That suggests Democrats might not be able to solely rely on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help overcome voters' concerns about the economy, border security and the age of Biden, who is 81.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What impact do you think the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue will have on the 2024 elections in Arizona? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Gail Nierad, 73, a retired nurse from Paradise Valley, will likely support GOP candidates in next year's election. At the same time, she said she would vote to expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because even though the candidates she is likely to back have more restrictive views on the topic, "I believe in it."\par \par \uc2 Kathy Kay, 76, another retiree from Scottsdale, is a registered Democrat who now votes Republican. She said she supports the current 15-week law but believes allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until viability goes too far. She will likely oppose the ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 Write to Eliza Collins at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:eliza.collins@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliza.collins@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231210ejca000p3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
