\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Wisconsin's Very Own Roe v. Wade? A judge finds an 1849  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  law doesn't ban  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  after all.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 459 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2023\par 22:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal again in Wisconsin, after a judge in Dane County held Tuesday that an 1849 law that had been thought to ban the procedure doesn't apply as long as the mother consents. This ruling could go to the state Supreme Court, and the liberal Justices there are already flexing their new 4-3 majority.\par \par \uc2 Circuit Judge Diane Schlipper had signaled this was coming in a July opinion, though the law isn't exactly confusing. "Any person, other than the mother, who intentionally destroys the life of an unborn child is guilty of a Class H felony," one section says. There's an exception for when an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 But Judge Schlipper found that the statute "prohibits feticide and not \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," citing a 1994 state Supreme Court case that involved a man who attacked his pregnant wife. Last week the judge stood by her analysis and granted a declaratory judgment that the law does not apply to consensual abortions.\par \par \uc2 Notably, however, Judge Schlipper declined to issue an injunction to shield \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers from prosecution. The counties in her courtroom, she wrote, pledged to abide by her ruling, and "any order to enjoin the conduct of the parties would only grant injunctive relief against three particular district attorneys." The upshot is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will now resume in Wisconsin even as Democrats retain it as a campaign issue, since their candidates could raise the specter of potential prosecutions in other counties, even if none ever actually happen.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin's 1849 law became a political flashpoint after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  returned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to the states in last year's Dobbs decision. Democrats capitalized on it this spring to flip the state Supreme Court to liberal control. New Justice Janet Protasiewicz explicitly made her election about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, galvanizing voters who objected to the state's total ban.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin's progressives would be loath to give up that political advantage, even if they are glad to see \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  up and running again. The group reopened clinics in Milwaukee and Dane counties in recent months after Judge Schlipper indicated that she would not enforce the law. Sheboygan County District Attorney Joel Urmanski has said he plans to appeal, which means this is probably headed for the state Supreme Court, exactly as Justice Protasiewicz advertised.\par \par \uc2 Be prepared for more legal contortions, even though the democratic answer is clear. Wisconsinites might want greater access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the 1849 law provided. If so, that's an adjustment properly made by state lawmakers who are accountable to voters.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231210ejca002e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
