\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Congress Moves Forward on Roughly $900 Billion Defense Bill; National Defense Authorization Act calls for 5.2% pay raise for service members\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katy Stech Ferek \par \uc2 1,374 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 December 2023\par 21:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14House and Senate lawmakers agreed to scrap the most contentious pieces of a nearly $900 billion defense and national security policy bill, such as restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and transgender care for service members, clearing the way for its passage by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers who serve on both chambers' armed services committees on Wednesday night released compromise text for the annual {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.house.gov/billsthisweek/20231211/FY24%20NDAA%20Conference%20Report%20-%20%20FINAL.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Defense Authorization Act }}}\uc2 . This year's NDAA, which lays out policy priorities for the Pentagon, calls for a 5.2% pay raise for service members and military training assistance to Taiwan to help the island defend itself against a possible invasion by China.\par \par \uc2 The text, called a conference report, came after House and Senate negotiators spent the past week hammering out differences between the NDAA bills their chambers passed earlier this year, a process that prompted House lawmakers to drop several controversial social-policy measures that were unlikely to get approval from President Biden and the Democratic-controlled Senate.\par \par \uc2 "Our nation faces unprecedented threats from China, Iran, Russia, and North Korea. It is vital that we act now to protect our national security," the four leaders of the armed services committees\u8212\'20\'14Sens. \uc2 Jack Reed\uc2  (D., R.I.), Roger Wicker (R., Miss.) and Reps. \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2  (R., Ala.) and \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2  (D., Wash.)\u8212\'20\'14said in a statement Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "Through months of hard-fought and productive negotiations, we have crafted a bipartisan and bicameral conference report that strengthens our national security and supports our service members," they said.\par \par \uc2 The newly released proposal now has to pass through both chambers and secure Biden's signature. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  (D., N.Y.) said the chamber's lawmakers should be prepared to vote on the bill next week.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers agreed to scrap what critics called culture-war provisions from the House version, including one that would have blocked the military's healthcare program from providing gender-related surgeries and hormone treatments for transgender people.\par \par \uc2 Another dropped provision from the House-passed bill would have prohibited the Defense Department from covering the costs related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would have invalidated a policy that Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  implemented after some states restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access following a Supreme Court ruling ending the right to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 That policy, which grants service members time off and travel money to get reproductive healthcare outside the states where they are stationed, infuriated some Republicans, including Sen. Tommy Tuberville (R., Ala.), who blocked hundreds of military promotions for months before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/tommy-tuberville-backs-down-in-fight-over-military-promotions-494cdbbc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 backing down this week }}}\uc2 . Some states with the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies are also home to major military installations including in Texas, Louisiana and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers did recommend a $70,000 pay cap for employees who work in the Defense Department's diversity, equity and inclusion offices along with a hiring freeze in those offices until Comptroller General officials release an audit of the divisions.\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  (R., Ga.) said she was "extremely disappointed" that the provisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and transgender care were dropped. She said that former Speaker Kevin McCarthy (R., Calif.) put her on the negotiating committee to fight for their inclusion but that her participation in coming up with the final text was unfairly limited.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker Mike Johnson (R., La.) said in a letter to colleagues that the negotiated agreement successfully cut out extraneous provisions, adding that the bill was thousands of pages shorter than last year.\par \par \uc2 The NDAA proposal, which would increase America's total national security budget by roughly 3% to $886 billion from last year's $858 billion authorization bill, contains hundreds of smaller policy proposals as lawmakers steer Defense Department officials toward priorities.\par \par \uc2 The proposal calls for roughly $170 billion to buy helicopters, combat vehicles, Navy submarines, weapons such as bombs and rockets, and a $1.9 billion, 684-foot-long ship to transport Marines, anchor overseas security operations and provide humanitarian aid to struggling regions. It also calls for an additional $145 billion for research and technology development.\par \par \uc2 The legislation would allow the U.S. to sell nuclear-powered submarines to Australia, despite tight capacity in the U.S. shipbuilding industry, but not until 2025. The transfer is meant to counter the influence of China's growing military.\par \par \uc2 Nuclear-powered submarines are far more capable than their conventional counterparts because they can operate stealthily underwater for great distances and long periods.\par \par \uc2 The proposal would send $300 million for security assistance to help fortify Ukraine's military after nearly two years of fighting against the Russian invasion. That is separate from the tens of billions of dollars in aid requested by Biden that failed to move forward Wednesday in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/border-fight-set-to-derail-ukraine-aid-vote-in-senate-a67e53c3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Senate procedural vote }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congress has so far approved more than $100 billion to help Kyiv. Some House Republicans have resisted sending more money, pressured by voters in their congressional districts who want lawmakers to give priority to domestic problems over a foreign conflict.\par \par \uc2 The proposed NDAA report includes several measures aimed at strengthening the U.S. military's ability to counter China, funding initiatives to keep relationships with allies in the Pacific region close while carrying out military exercises as a show of U.S. strength.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers have proposed to fund new technologies to better position the U.S. military to counter potential Chinese aggression, including high-energy lasers, mobile micronuclear reactors and radar sophisticated enough to track high-altitude balloons capable of spying on U.S. communications.\par \par \uc2 The proposal also requires defense officials to reach out to service members who were dismissed for refusing Covid-19 vaccines, which were once required. Congress removed that requirement in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ndaa-whats-in-the-858-billion-defense-policy-bill-11671124935"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last year's NDAA }}}\uc2  after Republicans said it could hurt recruitment.\par \par \uc2 The bill also extends until mid-April the government's foreign surveillance powers, which are due to lapse at the end of this year. The decision to essentially table until the spring what is expected to be a fierce security and privacy debate over those powers could lead some lawmakers to vote against the overall defense legislation. More than 50 House lawmakers from both parties recently wrote to congressional leadership saying they opposed any effort to temporarily extend Section 702 of the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act by linking it to the must-pass annual defense bill. Johnson said in his letter that the extension will give lawmakers more time to negotiate bipartisan changes to the law.\par \par \uc2 Absent from the legislation is a provision regulating American investment in certain Chinese technology sectors. The Senate overwhelmingly approved an amendment over the summer that would have required Americans to disclose their investments in some Chinese industries, and lawmakers had sought to include a similar plan in the NDAA.\par \par \uc2 But Republican opposition to the proposal in the House stymied those efforts. Some GOP lawmakers had warned that regulating outbound investment would harm American national security.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is already moving forward with an executive order that would restrict U.S. investment into Chinese advanced technology.\par \par \uc2 Also excluded from the report's recommendations is the reauthorization and expansion of the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act, a 1990 law that pays people who were exposed to radiation and experienced health problems including cancer. The current law expires in the spring.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  (R., Mo.), who proposed to expand the law's eligibility requirements to include several St. Louis neighborhoods where radioactive contamination has been found, has threatened to slow down the quick passage of the defense policy bill if the expansion wasn't included.\par \par \uc2 Some lawmakers had balked at including the reauthorization and expansion over its cost. The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  estimated it would cost $147 billion over 10 years, but Hawley told reporters earlier this week that he has managed to chop $100 billion off that estimated cost.\par \par \uc2 "The defense contractors will get paid\u8230\'20\'26but for the people of my state who are sick with cancer because of the government's nuclear waste, they get nothing," he said in a speech on the Senate floor on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Duehren and Dustin Volz contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Katy Stech Ferek at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:katy.stech@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 katy.stech@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231207ejc70015p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
