\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Don't Believe the Hype About  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 ; Its importance in last week's elections for state office has been vastly overstated.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karl Rove \par \uc2 832 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 November 2023\par 23:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 There's a tendency in politics to ascribe success and failure to one thing when it's really more complicated. That has been the case with most coverage of last week's elections. "{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/abortion-issues-burn-gop-democrats-big-election-night/story?id=104710552"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  issues burn GOP}}}\uc2 " screamed ABC News. "Democrats see big wins" shouted Roll Call, which said "{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rollcall.com/2023/11/08/democrats-see-big-wins-in-local-elections-look-to-2024/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 access to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 " was "front and center}}}\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The key example offered for this line of reasoning was Virginia's state legislative elections, in which Democrats held the Senate, losing only one seat, and flipped the House by picking up three. Vox's Rachel Cohen described the\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/2023/11/8/23952090/ohio-election-results-issue-1-abortion-virginia-kentucky-roe-dobbs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 results in Virginia and elsewhere}}}\uc2  as "a resounding victory for Democrats and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters." But is holding a 21-19 majority in the Senate and a 51-49 majority in the House really a resounding victory?\par \par \uc2 I think not. Virginia is a blue state that Mr. Biden carried 54% to 44% in 2020. Last week Republicans won in seven House districts Mr. Biden carried in 2020 by up to 10 points and four Senate districts he won by up to 9 points. Democrats didn't flip a single district \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took. These margins don't fit with the notion that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  draws large numbers of independents and Republicans to vote for Democratic candidates.\par \par \uc2 Two factors probably had a bigger effect than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The commonwealth was redistricted before the election. That {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.realclearpolitics.com/articles/2023/11/09/post-election_analysis_virginia_ohio_kentucky_and_mississippi_150035.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 benefited Democrats}}}\uc2  last week, according to Sean Trende, a senior election analyst at RealClearPolitics. Since Virginia is blue, the redistricting resulted in more solidly Democratic districts than solidly Republican ones. Mr. Trende was one of the special masters appointed by the Virginia Supreme Court to draw the lines.\par \par \uc2 Offsetting the Democratic redistricting advantage was the popularity of Virginia's Republican governor, Glenn Youngkin. An Oct. 16 Washington Post/Schar School poll {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2023/10/20/abortion-virginia-election-poll-youngkin-approval/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that 54% of Virginians approved of the governor's job performance and 39% disapproved. Only 43% of the same respondents thought Mr. Biden was doing a good job, while 55% didn't. This favorability advantage for Republicans helped GOP candidates grab districts that normally would have gone Democratic.\par \par \uc2 Given that their state has trended Democratic for years, Virginia Republicans held up admirably against a challenging map. The GOP would cheer if it pulled off similar margin changes in 2024. If Republicans flipped every U.S. House seat Democrats won by 10 points or less in 2022, the GOP would rack up 50 seats\u8212\'20\'14a 271-seat majority, the biggest GOP seat haul since 1928.\par \par \uc2 GOP Senate candidates would also be sitting pretty if they do as well in 2024 as Virginia Republican Senate hopefuls did last week. If GOP candidates won every state Mr. Biden carried by 9 points or less in 2020 and all the Trump states, there would be new Republican senators from Arizona, Michigan, Minnesota, Montana, Nevada, Ohio, Pennsylvania, West Virginia and Wisconsin\u8212\'20\'14giving the GOP a 58-seat majority.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates also point to Kentucky, where incumbent Democratic Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  defeated Trump-backed Republican Attorney General \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 , 52.5% to 47.5%. Ms. Cohen {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/2023/11/8/23952090/ohio-election-results-issue-1-abortion-virginia-kentucky-roe-dobbs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  that Mr. Beshear's re-election provides "the clearest evidence" that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drew "voters of all persuasions" to support Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Democrats did run a powerful ad featuring a young woman raped by her stepfather when she was 12. But if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone decided the governor's race, why is it that the rest of the statewide Republican ticket won with 57% to 61% of the vote and an average margin of 18 points? All those Republicans are strongly pro-life. Maybe they won because the governor's race was more about the incumbent's record and each candidate's messages on a range of issues, not only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the flip side, Ohio approved a ballot measure enshrining a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state constitution by 56.6% to 43.4%. But Republicans royally screwed up that situation. First, they tried to game the system with an August referendum raising the threshold for amending the state constitution, which voters soundly rejected. Then they didn't offer an alternative to the unlimited-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proposal and were massively outspent.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022, pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have gone seven for seven on state ballot measures. To {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/TheDemocrats/status/1722392809586446689?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 describe last week's results}}}\uc2  as "a huge sign of Democrats' continued momentum"\u8212\'20\'14in the words of a Democratic Party tweet\u8212\'20\'14and principally credit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to oversimplify. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have helped Democrats sometimes, but the issue is hardly a silver bullet. As Virginia showed, as long as Mr. Biden is the face of the party, pro-life candidates can make gains on Democratic turf if they frame the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue with care.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rove helped organize the political-action committee American Crossroads and is author of "The Triumph of William McKinley" (\uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , 2015).\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231115ejbf006mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
