\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 How Democrats Are Leveraging  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights to Win in 2024; Push to get  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  protections on the ballot promises to galvanize voters and affect races for the White House and control of Congress\b0\par\par\uc2 By Laura Kusisto and Jimmy Vielkind \par \uc2 1,287 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2023\par 02:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Fresh off {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/abortion-rights-supporters-rack-up-victories-putting-gop-in-bind-for-2024-4bec7b38"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 another round of victories }}}\uc2  in Tuesday's elections, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists are seeking to put the issue on the ballot in numerous states in 2024, a wild card that could influence next year's campaigns for the White House and control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 When Ohio voters this week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/election-day-ballots-ohio-abortion-maine-texas-colorado-d9922480"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed a measure }}}\uc2  to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the state constitution, it gave \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups seven consecutive victories in ballot initiatives since the Supreme Court last year {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminated a constitutional right to the procedure }}}\uc2 . Those successes have come in politically diverse states, from Kansas and Kentucky to Michigan and California.\par \par \uc2 Now, well-funded and organized efforts are under way to place \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiatives on the ballot in Arizona, which is poised to figure heavily in next year's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/whos-running-for-president-in-2024-adb754e2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 presidential election }}}\uc2 , and Florida. There are also nascent efforts to push referendums in states such as Colorado, Nebraska and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Then there is New York, where voters will consider an equal-rights amendment to the state constitution that includes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protections for \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access }}}\uc2 . The state, however, already has broad \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and it isn't clear if the measure will galvanize voters as it has elsewhere. New York Democrats hope the referendum will boost them after a poor showing in 2022 that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/wins-in-new-york-state-ease-republicans-path-to-house-majority-11668053723"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arguably cost the party control of the U.S. House }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How those efforts fare could color races up and down the ballot, potentially giving Democrats a weapon they have used to help energize their base and counter voter disenchantment with the economy and lack of enthusiasm for President Biden's re-election. Republicans, for their part, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-dobbs-year-after-roe-support-politics-d1ef5a5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have struggled to develop a winning message on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  since the Supreme Court's ruling.\par \par \uc2 While Democrats are eager to capitalize on the issue, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups don't want to overplay their hand and rush in with under-resourced efforts to tee-up new ballot initiatives they might lose. Even with momentum, initiative battles, which often require proponents to collect hundreds of thousands of signatures from registered voters to place an issue on the ballot, take an enormous investment of time and money, and competition for political fundraising and voter attention will be fierce in 2024. Recent ballot campaigns in some states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/the-abortion-rights-movement-is-on-a-winning-streak-will-ohio-stop-it-4c13d41f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have cost upward of $40 million }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I think that would be a real misread of the opportunity to try to do it everywhere. That's a big mistake," said Deirdre Schifeling, chief political and advocacy officer for \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Advocacy groups are pinning many of their ballot-initiative hopes for 2024 on Arizona. That state has some nearly ideal conditions for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups: restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws on the books, a sympathetic governor and attorney general and a relatively straightforward process for getting a measure on the ballot. The state also has an electorate receptive to messages about personal freedom that have proven to be a winning strategy elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 "When you can put it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/support-for-abortion-access-is-near-record-wsj-norc-poll-finds-6021c712"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in the context of freedoms }}}\uc2 , then you can build big winning coalitions," said Tory Gavito, president of Way to Win, a nonprofit that helps get Democratic candidates elected. "The ballot measures are a part of that strategy."\par \par \uc2 Arizona state law requires about 384,000 signatures to place an initiative before voters. Organizations leading the campaign are hoping to gather double that number to help steel themselves for a potential legal challenge by opponents. Those signatures must be submitted to the secretary of state by next summer.\par \par \uc2 Once a Republican bastion, Arizona has become a major political battleground over the last several cycles. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the state in 2016 but lost it to Biden in 2020. In addition to the presidency, the state will have competitive races for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , House and the state legislature next year. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure is on the ballot, it could help boost voter turnout and affect those contests.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups have been working since the spring to gather signatures, and organizers say they are on track with more than half of the number they need to collect ahead of a Feb. 1 deadline. But state dynamics also illustrate how activists face challenges in placing a referendum on the ballot even when they are well-organized on an issue that appears to enjoy popular support.\par \par \uc2 State Attorney General \uc2 Ashley Moody\uc2 , a Republican, has filed a challenge to the initiative with the Florida Supreme Court, arguing its language is vague and confusing.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proponents "have proposed an amendment to the Florida Constitution using a misleading ballot summary to trick voters into freezing in place a legal framework that conceals the amendment's potentially sweeping legal effects," Moody wrote in a legal brief filed last month.\par \par \uc2 If the court, which is filled with Republican appointees, agrees, such a ruling could prevent the measure from getting on the ballot next year.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups earlier this fall filed a petition to try to place a constitutional amendment strengthening protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the ballot. Under state law, it would also need to pass in two elections, in 2024 and 2026.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/vermont-is-one-step-closer-to-adding-abortion-rights-to-its-constitution-11644348231"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vermont }}}\uc2 , California and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/michigans-abortion-tensions-reflect-uncertain-future-in-battleground-states-11651834802"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Michigan }}}\uc2 last year became the first states to enshrine explicit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in their state constitutions. More than a dozen states have protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state law, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , and some state courts have also interpreted their constitutions to protect a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So far, voters have been most motivated in states where there was a real threat that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be tightly restricted. That was true in Ohio and Michigan, as well as in Kansas, where voters last year rejected a proposed state constitutional amendment to exclude protections for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 While California voters passed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights amendment last year, the issue didn't appear to have the same type of galvanizing effect or spillover impact in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access already was robust. Republicans still won several competitive U.S. House races there.\par \par \uc2 The same dynamic could play out next year in New York.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How do you expect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fare on the ballot in 2024? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 The idea for the referendum there, which supporters say would make laws restricting abortions unconstitutional, germinated for years mostly among lawmakers. State legislators in July of 2022 took the first required step to putting it on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 State Sen. \uc2 Liz Krueger\uc2 , a Manhattan Democrat who sponsored the amendment, said she purposefully chose to place the referendum on the ballot in 2024 instead of this year because turnout will be higher with a presidential election and several close races for the U.S. House on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 "Voters in New York state are going to the ballot box both for this constitutional amendment but also against people who don't support these rights," she said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been legal under New York law since 1970, and lawmakers in 2019 strengthened \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a new statute.\par \par \uc2 Iona University political science professor Jeanne Sheehan Zaino said other voter concerns likely would be more salient. "In New York, while you can scare people on certain things regarding their right to choose and what's happening elsewhere, they're not going to be as fearful of that as they are on issues of crime, security and things like inflation," she said.\par \par \uc2 Eliza Collins contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Laura.Kusisto@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Jimmy Vielkind at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jimmy.vielkind@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jimmy.vielkind@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231111ejbb0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
