\par\par \uc2 Election 2024\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Democrats Grow More Confident in Campaign Message, but More Nervous About Biden; Some in party worry the president will drag them down in 2024, even as Tuesday's elections show  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  issues can motivate voters Democrats need to win\b0\par\par\uc2 By Annie Linskey, Ken Thomas and Katy Stech Ferek \par \uc2 1,715 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par 21:15 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14A year away from the 2024 election, Democrats see trends pulling in different directions: an electorate that appears motivated to vote in their favor, particularly on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but is also deeply skeptical of handing another term to the party's standard-bearer\u8212\'20\'14President Biden.\par \par \uc2 The 80-year-old incumbent faced flashing red warning lights this week in the form of fresh polling showing voters' concerns about his age and weak numbers in swing-state matchups against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But Biden's team and allies see no need for a course correction, pointing to the power of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics and the value of having Trump as a foil.\par \par \uc2 Democrats said they were vindicated by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/abortion-rights-supporters-rack-up-victories-putting-gop-in-bind-for-2024-4bec7b38"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tuesday's off-year election results }}}\uc2 , in which voters rejected efforts to curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and re-elected a Democratic governor in Kentucky despite attempts to link him to Biden's first-term agenda.\par \par \uc2 "Democrats are on the right page as it relates to the needs and desires of our families and our citizens," said Rep. \uc2 Bonnie Watson Coleman\uc2  (D., N.J.). "It's just a matter of connecting our substance with our narrative."\par \par \uc2 Others are less sure, with some Democrats across the country harboring doubts about whether Biden is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/why-third-party-candidates-threaten-biden-in-2024-b639e64f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the strongest candidate }}}\uc2  to take on Trump, the likely GOP nominee, even as many acknowledge that the president appears locked into running. Their fears were underlined in recent days by polling and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/davidaxelrod/status/1721191649647198643?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by a warning }}}\uc2  from David Axelrod, a former strategist to former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who raised the idea that the party should consider changing horses.\par \par \uc2 Interviews with a dozen Democratic leaders in five swing states found that they registered varying degrees of concern about the president's ability to defeat Trump, and some questioned the wisdom of putting him forward again as their nominee.\par \par \uc2 "It feels like he was the perfect person for 2020. But not for 2024," said one former Democratic state lawmaker in Michigan, a key battleground. "He's a transitional president and you have to know when to transition and it is now."\par \par \uc2 Another potential problem for Biden emerged Thursday when Jill Stein {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DrJillStein/status/1722641795568259561?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced }}}\uc2  she plans to seek the Green Party's nomination for president. Her announcement video highlighted her support for a cease-fire in Gaza, an issue that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/democrats-risk-long-lasting-rift-over-israel-hamas-war-ec74044f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has roiled the Democratic Party }}}\uc2 in recent weeks. In 2016, many Democrats believed that she cost Hillary Clinton the presidential election by siphoning votes in Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Stein joins a growing field of third-party candidates who could scramble the vote in 2024, with environmental attorney \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  and academic Cornel West both running as independents.\par \par \uc2 Also on Thursday, Sen. Joe Manchin (D., W.Va.) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/democratic-sen-joe-manchin-wont-run-for-re-election-in-west-virginia-5f3e2fbf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced he wouldn't run }}}\uc2  for another term in the Senate. He is viewed as a possible presidential pick for the centrist group No Labels.\par \par \uc2 The White House instead wanted to focus on the results from Tuesday's elections. In the three most closely watched states Tuesday turned on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, an issue the White House has embraced but with which Biden has been personally uncomfortable. Democrats won full control of the Virginia state house in a year where Republicans bet big on taking control of both chambers. Voters in Ohio, a state Biden lost by 8 percentage points in 2020, enshrined \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the state's constitution. In deep-red Kentucky, Democratic Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , 45, won re-election after running ads on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Democrats and Biden's campaign pointed to the races as proof that Biden's message and agenda are popular. But some skeptics said those victories couldn't be attributed to Biden, who wasn't publicly involved in any of the contests. Biden's sagging poll numbers, and the unease from Democratic leaders across the country, rest largely on an attribute he can do little to correct: his age, and the perception among voters that he is frail.\par \par \uc2 Biden's own problems, GOP strategist Liam Donovan {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LPDonovan/status/1722129384151093705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said on X }}}\uc2 , formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , after Tuesday's election, are why "a good night that otherwise calms Dem nerves nonetheless ratchets up the central contradiction\u8212\'20\'14despite broader success and a favorable environment, the President himself seems uniquely vulnerable."\par \par \uc2 Biden turns 81 this month. Trump is 77, but voters don't tend to have the same concerns about his age, polls show.\par \par \uc2 Rick Bloomingdale, a \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  member from Pennsylvania and retired labor leader, said that in discussions with friends, including Republicans who don't like Trump, there is a consistent worry about Biden's age. "They're like, 'Biden is too old,'" he said.\par \par \uc2 Bloomingdale said he argues that voters should judge the president by his accomplishments instead, but it can be tricky. "Biden is not much of a horn-blower," Bloomingdale said. "Democrats are good at doing. We're not as good at telling the story as we should."\par \par \uc2 Surveys consistently show voters disapproving of Biden's handling of the economy and giving Trump more credit on his economic record compared with Biden. Trump has repeatedly pointed to the state of the U.S. economy before the Covid-19 pandemic and assailed Biden for elevated levels of inflation. That has prompted some Democrats to urge the president to make a sharper comparison with Trump on the economy.\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  (D., Calif.) said the election results showed that Democrats had a clear advantage on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and divisive issues such as banning books in school. But he said Democrats "still have work to do" on their economic pitch to voters and pointed to President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's 1996 re-election campaign as a model.\par \par \uc2 "They didn't run a campaign saying, 'Thank you, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 .' They ran a campaign saying, 'No way, Newt Gingrich,'" Khanna said of the party's 1996 campaign, referring to the polarizing GOP House Speaker. "And I think the president needs to say that here's what the Republicans will do."\par \par \uc2 Others were more blunt in their assessments.\par \par \uc2 "The sense I get from people is that they are hoping the president will make the decision that it's in the best interest of the country that a stronger candidate be the Democratic Party's nominee. I don't think anyone is prepared to push him out," said one DNC member, who spoke on condition of anonymity. "At some point, he may have no choice."\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2023/11/07/politics/cnn-poll-trump-biden-matchup/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNN survey }}}\uc2  released this week showed Trump with a slight edge over Biden among registered voters nationwide. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/11/05/us/elections/times-siena-battlegrounds-registered-voters.html?unlocked_article_code=1.8Ew.PEVT.yLDez68a2s5k"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times/Siena College poll }}}\uc2  surveyed six battleground states and showed Trump ahead in Nevada, Georgia, Arizona, Michigan and Pennsylvania. It showed Biden slightly ahead in Wisconsin. The weak numbers come despite the Biden campaign and the DNC having already spent millions of dollars on advertising in those states touting the president's record.\par \par \uc2 Even more worrying for Democrats, both surveys indicated that chunks of Biden's coalition are dissolving with the president receiving lower levels of support from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/black-voters-show-signs-of-slipping-away-from-biden-in-2024-69ae78b0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voters who are young, Black or Hispanic }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both also showed that Biden's age is a problem. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  poll found that 71% of voters think Biden is too old to be president, a number that has climbed from 34% who said he was too old when asked in 2020. Thirty-nine percent said that Trump is too old to be president, up from 18% who said so in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The White House and Biden allies dismissed the polling as a mere snapshot in time and said that both Clinton and Obama had poor showings a year ahead of their eventual re-elections.\par \par \uc2 Asked Thursday if he thinks he is trailing in battleground states, the president said: "No, I don't."\par \par \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , the 2016 Democratic presidential nominee, said Wednesday on ABC's "The View" that until nominees are chosen, "People are always saying, 'Well, maybe there's somebody better out there, let me look over here,' and 'Oh, I see somebody better out there.' That's natural." She added that Biden has shown he can do a good job, particularly when compared with Trump's tenure in the White House.\par \par \uc2 After announcing his re-election bid in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ChjibtX0UzU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April via a video }}}\uc2 , Biden has focused mostly on fundraising. The president has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/biden-tops-trump-and-other-takeaways-from-latest-2024-campaign-fundraising-1d1583e9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outraised Trump significantly }}}\uc2  in the past two quarters, amassing a $91 million war chest with Democratic partners.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats want to see more on-the-ground activity from the campaign, with a sharper message. Rep. Steven Horsford (D., Nev.) said he had encouraged Biden "to focus on centering the people\u8212\'20\'14not the politics, not the process. The campaign needs to step up its game and how it's engaging with voters in states like Nevada."\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Do the Democrats have a winning strategy in place for 2024? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 "There's a sentiment that more needs to be done," Horsford added, explaining that some of Biden's accomplishments, including lowering the cost of some prescription drugs, aren't widely known.\par \par \uc2 "The energy is what's lacking," said Rep. Ra\u250\'00\'FAl Grijalva (D., Ariz.), assessing Biden's issues connecting with voters, which he thinks will be overcome. "And that comes from having a field organization and that also comes as more and more candidates begin to gear up."\par \par \uc2 Biden's campaign team and DNC leaders say they are on track to build a winning organization and said that early fretting about whether Biden is getting credit for his accomplishments is misplaced.\par \par \uc2 "I will take the point that we have work to do to ingrain those accomplishments in the lives of everyday voters," said Sam Cornale, the executive director of the DNC. "But that is exactly what a campaign\u8212\'20\'14and this campaign specifically\u8212\'20\'14is designed to do. And we will do it, and he will win."\par \par \uc2 Eliza Collins contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Annie Linskey at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:annie.linskey@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 annie.linskey@wsj.com }}}\uc2 , Ken Thomas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:ken.thomas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ken.thomas@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Katy Stech Ferek at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:katy.stech@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 katy.stech@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231109ejb9002gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
