\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Ohio Voters Enshrine  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access in State Constitution; Ballot initiative was test of where voters stand on  \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kris Maher and Jon Kamp \par \uc2 1,457 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2023\par 05:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ohio voters decided Tuesday to put {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/abortion-rights-supporters-rack-up-victories-putting-gop-in-bind-for-2024-4bec7b38"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights }}}\uc2  in the Midwestern state's constitution, one of several states where the issue resonated with voters and showed its potential to help Democrats next year.\par \par \uc2 The Ohio vote was another key test of where voters stand on one of the most consequential issues heading into next year's presidential election. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate also played into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/election-day-2023-gubernatorial-races-247b7d34?mod=hp_lead_pos8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tuesday victories for Democrats }}}\uc2 , who held their majority in the Virginia Senate and wrestled the majority from Republicans in the state's House of Delegates. Also, Democrat \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  won another term as governor of Kentucky, where he criticized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions passed by the legislature.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-dobbs-year-after-roe-support-politics-d1ef5a5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  more than a year ago, voters had defeated ballot measures that would have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 restricted \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights }}}\uc2  in other red states such as Kansas and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 The Ohio vote was the toughest referendum fight yet, however. For the first time, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates were trying to pass protections in a solidly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/republican-debate-miami-what-to-expect-ab891722"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican-leaning }}}\uc2  state. The Associated Press called the race after the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights ballot measure took a comfortable lead during vote counting.\par \par \uc2 In a statement praising the passage of the Ohio ballot measure, President Biden said efforts by Republicans across the country to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access were out of step with the vast majority of Americans. "Tonight, Americans once again voted to protect their fundamental freedoms\u8212\'20\'14and democracy won," he said.\par \par \uc2 The measure will establish a state constitutional right to make one's own reproductive decisions, including about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It will allow the state to prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after fetal viability, which is typically around 22 to 24 weeks, except when a woman's life or health is at risk.\par \par \uc2 Shaylynn Michael, who works in retail sales for a cellphone company in Steubenville, Ohio, said she voted in favor of the ballot measure. The 26-year-old Democrat said her son was born shortly after Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 "As someone who had just had a child, I couldn't imagine if something were to be medically wrong that could endanger my life not being able to have a medical procedure to save my life," she said. "I just don't believe that other people should have the right to decide what someone does with their body."\par \par \uc2 Other voters in Steubenville, in a former steelmaking region in eastern Ohio close to Pennsylvania and West Virginia, said they opposed the ballot measure, known as Issue 1. Many cited their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on religious grounds or said they believed the issue should be handled through legislation rather than a constitutional amendment.\par \par \uc2 Married couple Greg Rayburn and Christa Rayburn, 58 and 48, respectively, said they were worried the ballot measure could lead to minors receiving abortions without parental consent. They both voted no.\par \par \uc2 "I have three teenage daughters," said Christa Rayburn, who like her husband is a Republican. "Even if they didn't follow my wishes, it would break my heart that I couldn't be there to support them."\par \par \uc2 In another notable contest, the AP said voters in Maine rejected a measure to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/maine-election-ballot-measure-power-company-d46ba54f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 create a new consumer-owned electric utility }}}\uc2  by forcing a buyout of two investor-owned companies that currently cover most of the New England state. This was the second-most-expensive ballot fight on Tuesday after the Ohio \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  vote, fueled by spending from two investor-owned utilities that fought to keep their jobs, according to Ballotpedia, a nonpartisan election site.\par \par \uc2 The Ohio and Maine contests were among more than two dozen ballot initiatives in five states on Tuesday, with other questions ranging from whether to legalize marijuana to how to spend tax money from cigarette sales. Texas has the most such measures with 14, including several that could provide billions of dollars in funding for new power plants, state parks, water infrastructure and broadband projects.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Ohio also decided Tuesday to make recreational marijuana legal in the state. Marijuana had been legal in 23 states, with most of those approving its use through ballot measures.\par \par \uc2 Ohioans expressed stronger views about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure, and many were already looking ahead to next year's presidential contest.\par \par \uc2 The Rayburns, who voted against the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure, said they both plan to support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Christa Rayburn said she believes the many legal cases against the former president are politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 "If all of these things are true against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , why didn't they do this seven years ago?" she said. "It's because there's an election coming up and they really want to keep him off the ballot."\par \par \uc2 Toni Hubbard, a 76-year-old pastor who also voted against the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure, said she would support President Biden. She said she thinks attacks on his age are misplaced.\par \par \uc2 Hubbard, a Democrat, said she fears a Trump victory next year. "He will tear down the country if he's in there," she said.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio's high-stakes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fight, campaigns on both sides of the issue received more than $71 million in contributions, with supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights receiving $41 million, compared with roughly $30 million for opponents, according to Ballotpedia.\par \par \uc2 Conservative groups centered much of their advertising campaign on the issues of parental consent and late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What ballot initiatives have you been following most closely this election cycle? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 The Ohio amendment doesn't include language that would change the state's parental-consent law, and currently minors must get their parents' approval for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But a legal analysis by the state's Republican attorney general said "there is no guarantee" that the current parental-consent law would remain in effect if the amendment is passed. Opponents had used the issue to galvanize voters.\par \par \uc2 "It is the strongest messaging by far," said Michael Gonidakis, president of Ohio Right to Life, before Tuesday's vote. "When you mess around with parents and their kids, parents unite to stop it\u8212\'20\'14even pro-choice parents."\par \par \uc2 Gonidakis and others had said that the proposed amendment would open the door to people under the age of 18 obtaining abortions without their parents being notified and consenting. Legal scholars said that would only happen if the state's existing parental-consent law is struck down in the courts.\par \par \uc2 "What the opposition is trying to do is insert other strategies like parental rights into the conversation to distract from their ultimate goal of a national ban of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , before the vote.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups said that Republicans in the state used their political power to try to deceive voters about the measure in other ways, such as by changing the summary language of the amendment to "whether an unborn child is viable," rather than a reference to fetal viability in the wording used in the draft submitted by proponents.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's ballot question followed one pushed by antiabortion groups in August\u8212\'20\'14 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ohio-election-abortion-issue-one-vote-d7df6402"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which Ohio voters rejected }}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14that would have made it harder to amend the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 An Ohio law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at six weeks has been held up by a court injunction and currently doctors can perform abortions in the state up until about 22 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Some voters said they supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure, but still believe the procedure should be performed in limited situations.\par \par \uc2 In Steubenville, Maryann Bush-Jeter said she is a Christian and a nurse and decided to vote in favor of Issue 1 because she believes there are times, such as when a pregnant woman's life is in jeopardy, when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think it should be used as a means of birth control. No, I do not," she said.\par \par \uc2 Major Ballot Measures\par \par \uc2 * Colorado Approve property tax changes Allow state to retain surplus revenue from taxes on cigarettes\par \par \uc2 * Maine Create consumer-owned utility Prohibit election spending by foreign governments Allow access to vehicle on-board diagnostic systems for repairs\par \par \uc2 * New York Remove debt limits for small city school districts\par \par \uc2 * Ohio Provide each individual the right to make and carry out reproductive decisions Legalize recreational use of marijuana for adults\par \par \uc2 * Texas Create or alter funds to support: \u8212\'20\'14higher education research \u8212\'20\'14water infrastructure \u8212\'20\'14gas-fueled power plants \u8212\'20\'14broadband infrastructure \u8212\'20\'14creation/maintenance of state parks Prohibit the legislature from enacting a "wealth tax" in the future Increase a tax exemption for property taxes\par \par \uc2 Laura Kusisto and Julie Bykowicz contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Kris Maher at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Kris.Maher@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kris.Maher@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Jon Kamp at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Jon.Kamp@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jon.Kamp@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020231107ejb70025y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
