\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 The  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 -Rights Movement Is on a Winning Streak. Will Ohio Stop It? Tuesday's elections will test whether Democrats can keep benefiting from the issue in 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Bykowicz and Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,676 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2023\par 10:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the aftermath of the Supreme Court's decision {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to overturn Roe. v. Wade}}}\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters have put their opponents on defense by spending more money on advocacy, passing state-level protections through ballot measures and helping elect Democrats.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, two states will test whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights movement can keep up its momentum. In Ohio, a ballot measure will ask voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/election-day-ballots-ohio-abortion-maine-texas-colorado-d9922480"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to protect \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access}}}\uc2 , while in Virginia, the issue could help decide which party controls the state legislature.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/gop-voters-urge-2024-candidates-to-hold-firm-on-abortion-limits-8e8a455e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to feature prominently}}}\uc2  in the 2024 elections. Efforts are under way in at least six states to ask voters next year to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-dobbs-year-after-roe-support-politics-d1ef5a5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 consider \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 's legality}}}\uc2 . That includes Arizona, which could be pivotal to controlling both the White House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court's Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision wiped out Roe's federal protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some 16 states with Republican legislatures {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/roe-v-wade-is-gone-but-abortions-are-on-the-rise-383ffaf4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have banned or sharply restricted}}}\uc2  the procedure. Yet in five states where voters have been asked to weigh in directly, including Republican-heavy Kansas and Kentucky, they have favored \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will dominate elections for the foreseeable future, contrasting the issue's resonance with concerns about inflation and Republican infighting in Congress. "People don't often forget when their rights have been taken away," she said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-rights-supporters-prevail-in-midterm-ballot-measures-11667986139"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gave Democrats a significant boost}}}\uc2  in last year's midterms, raising confidence among \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists and candidates alike. Antiabortion organizations, by contrast, are struggling to develop a politically attractive message and raise the $100 million or more they plan to spend next year.\par \par \uc2 "We've never been good at the state level," said Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, an antiabortion group. "We have a very diverse country. It makes sense that each state deciding what to do about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a cumbersome process with fits and starts."\par \par \uc2 "That's what I say to our team, and to candidates who are freaked out," she said.\par \par \uc2 As antiabortion groups work to turn the tide, they have piled millions of dollars into Ohio, spending more than they have on any state ballot initiative since Dobbs. They are still being outspent by more than two to one, campaign finance reports filed at the end of October show.\par \par \uc2 It is perhaps the toughest battle yet for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights proponents because it is their first attempt since Dobbs to pass protections for abortions in a more conservative state. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents argue that voter passions have cooled since that decision more than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 One thing "no one anticipated was how much the fear of change would dominate that first year post-Roe," said Dave Zanotti, chief executive of the Ohio-based conservative American Policy Roundtable. "The climate of 2023," he added, " is profoundly different than immediately post-Dobbs."\par \par \uc2 The Ohio ballot measure would enshrine in the state constitution the right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout much of pregnancy, preventing the Republican-dominated legislature from passing laws that strictly limit the procedure. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters are optimistic: Ohio voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/ohio-election-abortion-issue-one-vote-d7df6402"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rebuffed an effort}}}\uc2  largely funded by antiabortion groups this August to make constitutional amendments harder to pass.\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, Democrats trying to hold on to control of the state Senate and flip the Republican house of delegates have made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the heart of their advertising campaigns, far outpacing other topics such as healthcare, guns and the economy.\par \par \uc2 Portraying themselves as a crucial block on potential restrictions or a total ban in a state led by Republican Gov. Glenn Youngkin, Democratic candidates have aired more than 38,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -relation spots at a cost of roughly $14.5 million, an analysis by AdImpact shows. By contrast, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  barely registers as a topic in Republican ads, which instead largely focus on crime and policing.\par \par \uc2 One of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://host2.adimpact.com/admo/#/viewer/1182c053-1e17-456e-8f22-bfecec98becb/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most frequently aired ads}}}\uc2  in recent weeks features Democratic House candidate Josh Cole saying, "If you listen to her story, a woman facing an impossible decision to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you'd know taking away her right to control her own body is just wrong."\par \par \uc2 That sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights echo chamber means that already well-funded groups are benefiting from a message that is being amplified broadly in the Democratic Party. Before the fall of Roe, Democrats had a far less cohesive approach to talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, often preferring to skip the topic altogether.\par \par \uc2 One place to see that increased confidence is in heavily Republican Kentucky, where Democratic Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/kentucky-governors-race-offers-test-can-a-popular-democratic-incumbent-win-in-trump-country-15c93d29"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seeking re-election}}}\uc2  this November.\par \par \uc2 The end of Roe triggered a Kentucky law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except when a doctor deems the woman's life to be at risk. But last November, by a margin of 4 percentage points, voters rejected a ballot measure that would have explicitly stated that the Kentucky constitution doesn't protect a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Emboldened by that voter feedback, Beshear has repeatedly criticized his opponent, Republican Attorney General Daniel Cameron, for defending the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the courts, and called him out for refusing to clearly answer whether he believes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal for victims of rape and incest. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich427\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6405
