\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , Texas Spar Over Crisis Pregnancy Center Listings; Texas attorney general says company mischaracterized centers in consumer notices; \uc2 Yelp\uc2  says its statements are accurate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Calfas \par \uc2 724 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 September 2023\par 19:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Yelp\uc2  and Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  are suing each other over how the review site described crisis pregnancy centers in the months after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fall of Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Paxton filed a lawsuit in a Texas district court against \uc2 Yelp\uc2  on Thursday, alleging consumer notices once posted on the company's pages for crisis pregnancy centers were misleading. Anticipating this move, the tech company pre-emptively sued Paxton Wednesday in federal court in San Francisco, arguing its statements about crisis pregnancy centers are accurate and protected by the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 The legal challenges stem from the Supreme Court's Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision in June 2022, which eliminated federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/abortion-state-guide-laws-bf5072da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sent the issue back to the states }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-dobbs-year-after-roe-support-politics-d1ef5a5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 landmark decision }}}\uc2  drew attention to crisis pregnancy centers, which are typically nonprofits that counsel women against abortions. Supporters of the centers say they provide important services for pregnant women, and some state legislatures and antiabortion groups have sought to bolster support for the centers over the past year. Critics say the centers can be misleading about the services they provide\u8212\'20\'14particularly for women seeking abortions, who may arrive at centers believing those services are available.\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court's decision, \uc2 Yelp\uc2  added a consumer notice on its pages for crisis pregnancy centers, stating they "typically provide limited medical services and may not have licensed medical professionals onsite."\par \par \uc2 A group of two dozen attorneys general, including Paxton, sent a letter to \uc2 Yelp\uc2  in February arguing the language was misleading. The company said the information was accurate. It later changed the notices to read: "Crisis Pregnancy Centers do not offer abortions or referrals to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers."\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Yelp\uc2  has changed the language on its site, Paxton said in his complaint Thursday the first consumer notices violated the state's Deceptive Trade Practices Act. He said the company "remains liable for penalties and other relief for the duration of its unlawful behavior."\par \par \uc2 The state's complaint said the company unfairly targeted crisis pregnancy centers with the disclaimers and instead caused more confusion among consumers. It cited \uc2 Yelp\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Jeremy Stoppelman\uc2 's statement supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and calling on the company to take action after Politico published {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a leaked draft of the Dobbs opinion }}}\uc2  in May 2022.\par \par \uc2 "Major companies cannot abuse their platforms and influence to control consumers' behavior, especially on sensitive health issues like pregnancy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Paxton said in a statement Thursday.\par \par \uc2 A lawyer for \uc2 Yelp\uc2  declined to comment Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Yelp\uc2  has maintained that its consumer notices on crisis pregnancy centers throughout the past year are accurate. The intention, the company has said, is to help prevent consumers from being misled or confused by the services offered at these centers.\par \par \uc2 "It's been well-reported that crisis pregnancy centers do not offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services or referrals to them, and that many provide misleading information in an attempt to steer people seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care to other options\u8212\'20\'14this often starts with an online search," a \uc2 Yelp\uc2  spokesperson said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Other tech companies have grappled with how to label crisis pregnancy centers since last year. After representatives from \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's union urged the company to remove search results for crisis pregnancy centers, \uc2 Google\uc2  said in August 2022 it would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/google-says-maps-searches-will-identify-clinics-that-provide-abortions-11661457587"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 update its labeling system }}}\uc2  to add details like "provides abortions" and "might not provide abortions."\par \par \uc2 Yelp\uc2  said Paxton's office recently informed the company he planned to sue.\par \par \uc2 A Paxton spokeswoman said \uc2 Yelp\uc2 's pre-emptive lawsuit, filed in California, "is an improper attempt to avoid adjudication of a Texas state law claim, in a Texas state court, by the Texas Attorney General."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Yelp\uc2  spokesperson said Paxton "is concededly targeting \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , a San Francisco based company, for its nationwide speech," and the federal court in California is the best venue for the case.\par \par \uc2 In its complaint, the company said an injunction was needed "to avert an irreparable chill on \uc2 Yelp\uc2 's First Amendment rights, and free \uc2 Yelp\uc2  from either forgoing its rights or facing prosecution for exercising them." The complaint also noted Paxton called \uc2 Yelp\uc2 's updated labels for these centers "accurate."\par \par \uc2 Write to Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jennifer.calfas@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230928ej9s0058x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
