\par\par \uc2 National Security\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 CQ Brown Confirmed as Chairman of Joint Chiefs After Monthslong Holdup; Sen. Tuberville's hold on hundreds of other military promotions remains in place\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nancy A. Youssef and Lindsay Wise \par \uc2 964 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2023\par 23:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Senate sidestepped {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/tommy-tuberville-goes-on-defense-over-hold-on-military-promotions-racial-comments-235c8ed2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a monthslong blockade }}}\uc2  to confirm the incoming chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff and was on track to approve the top generals in charge of the Army and Marine Corps as well.\par \par \uc2 But hundreds of other senior officers' promotions remained frozen in a political battle over the Pentagon's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 The Senate late Wednesday voted 83-11 to confirm Air Force Gen. CQ Brown, the current chief of staff of the Air Force, as the next chairman, to succeed Army Gen. \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 , whose term expires Sept. 30.\par \par \uc2 In addition, senators are set to vote Thursday to confirm Army Gen. Randy George as the next chief of staff of the Army and Marine Corps Gen. Eric Smith as commandant. George's predecessor, Army Gen. James McConville, was statutorily required to retire in August, and George, the current vice chief of staff, has led the Army in an acting role. Smith has been the acting commandant since July when Gen. David Berger was also required to step down.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Tommy Tuberville (R., Ala.) started blocking the promotions for hundreds of military generals and admirals earlier this year, vowing not to let the Senate expedite their confirmations until the Pentagon agreed to end its policy of allowing troops leave and travel funds for reproductive healthcare, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tuberville has said he won't lift his hold on the promotions until the military changes its policy or Congress passes a measure codifying the policy.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, he stressed that his hold is still in place, in a speech on the Senate floor. "The hold will remain in place, as long as the Pentagon's illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remains in place," Tuberville said.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  (D., N.Y.) teed up the votes on Brown, George and Smith Wednesday after Tuberville said he planned to use a procedural maneuver, known as a cloture petition, to force a vote on just Smith's nomination.\par \par \uc2 It is extremely rare for a rank-and-file senator, especially from the minority party, to use such a petition to call up a nomination for a vote. If successful, it could have undermined longtime Senate practice, which gives the majority leader control over when bills and nominations come to the floor.\par \par \uc2 "These men should have already been confirmed," Schumer said in announcing the votes on Brown, George and Smith on Wednesday. "The Senate should not have to go through procedural hoops just to please one brazen and misguided senator. But this is where we are."\par \par \uc2 Schumer said the standoff has forced senators to weigh possible changes to the way the Senate processes military nominations.\par \par \uc2 While senators sometimes put holds on Pentagon political appointees who have policy-making responsibilities, the Tuberville move broke with Senate tradition as it applies to career military officers who aren't responsible for making policy. It led to the first time in military history that several services didn't have a confirmed leader, and more than 300 generals and admirals are still awaiting Senate confirmation, with no clear resolution in sight.\par \par \uc2 "Good that these three are potentially moving forward," said John Kirby, a spokesman for the National Security Council, ahead of the votes. "But it ain't good enough for U.S. national security\u8212\'20\'14these holds need to be lifted."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Congressional Research Service\uc2  in August estimated it would take about 700 hours to approve all the pending military nominations affected by Tuberville's holds. The number of affected nominees has only grown since then.\par \par \uc2 Tuberville has repeatedly said he doesn't believe the holds are affecting military readiness and that the government shouldn't be paying for anything related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Democrats say the lack of options for women on reproductive services would hurt readiness and be unfair for service members who don't choose where they are deployed.\par \par \uc2 With Milley's term set to expire at the end of the month, there has been growing pressure on Capitol Hill to break the impasse, with some Republican senators working behind the scenes to find a compromise.\par \par \uc2 Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  (R., Ky.) publicly chastised Tuberville, saying that his strategy was a mistake. But he didn't offer any solutions.\par \par \uc2 As recently as Monday, Tuberville said he wasn't feeling any pressure from his colleagues, and that Senate Democrats could have moved earlier to confirm Brown and other nominees, one by one.\par \par \uc2 "They're the ones who've dragged their feet on this, trying to prove a point that, you know, we shouldn't be doing this," Tuberville said.\par \par \uc2 At the Pentagon, civilian and military leaders alike have said that because of the holds, generals, admirals and their families don't know whether they can move or not to new positions. Even those not promoted were affected, the Pentagon said, because they often had to do two jobs, as generals and admirals retired and the positions had no successors in place.\par \par \uc2 Every few weeks, as commanders' terms expired, their photos on the leadership wall of the Pentagon were replaced with a blank slate, a physical display of missing leadership within the ranks.\par \par \uc2 Yet, while top Pentagon civilian leaders said the hold harmed national security, they didn't aggressively push Capitol Hill to end the hold. Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , who signed off on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, didn't regularly engage with legislators. He spoke to Tuberville a handful of times, defense officials said.\par \par \uc2 Gordon Lubold contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Nancy A. Youssef at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:nancy.youssef@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nancy.youssef@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Lindsay Wise at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:lindsay.wise@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lindsay.wise@wsj.com }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230920ej9k007ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
