\par\par \uc2 Main Street\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Owes Pro-Lifers; Trump made Dobbs possible\u8212\'20\'14but foes of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  made his presidency possible.\b0\par\par\uc2 By William McGurn \par \uc2 867 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par 22:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's interview with NBC's Kristen Welker aired Sunday morning on "Meet the Press," he was criticized by some pro-lifers. Some didn't like his calling the six-week ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  signed by Florida Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  a "terrible thing," while others took issue with his muddied answer on federal vs. state bans. Mr. Trump no doubt sees himself as he posted on Truth Social posted back in May:\par \par \uc2 "After 50 years of failure, with nobody coming even close, I was able to kill Roe v. Wade, much to the 'shock' of everyone, and for the first time put the Pro Life movement in a strong negotiating position over the Radicals that are willing to kill babies even into their 9th month, and beyond. Without me there would be no 6 weeks, 10 weeks, 15 weeks, or whatever is finally agreed to. Without me the pro Life movement would have just kept losing. Thank you President TRUMP!!!"\par \par \uc2 No one can deny Mr. Trump's achievement with Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization. His Supreme Court picks provided the margin of victory for overturning Roe v. Wade. He's also right that absent Dobbs there would be no bans at all. But he is on shakier ground when he says that without him "the pro Life movement would just have kept losing."\par \par \uc2 It's the other way around. Mr. Trump would never have been elected president without the active support of the pro-life and conservative legal movements. He had the wit to follow their lead.\par \par \uc2 In 2016 when Mr. Trump first ran for president, nobody knew what he believed. So he asked \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  at the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  to produce a list of jurists who would interpret the Constitution as it was written and as the Founders understood it. Candidate Trump pledged to choose from this list for his Supreme Court nominees.\par \par \uc2 So Mr. Trump deserves credit for the Court's Dobbs decision. But Dobbs itself was the culmination of a nearly 50-year effort to place on the court justices who understood the constitutional travesty Roe represented. Over those five decades, often against long odds, pro-lifers provided the critical support needed to get these justices confirmed and the presidents who nominated them elected.\par \par \uc2 It helps to go back to February 2016, when the great \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  died. In March, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  nominated \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to fill his seat. But Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  announced that the Senate wouldn't hold hearings so close to a presidential election, killing Mr. Garland's chances and keeping the seat open for the winner of the November election to fill.\par \par \uc2 That was the situation as Mr. Trump was moving closer to the GOP nomination. Mr. McConnell told him he needed to shore up support from Republicans worried about the Supreme Court\u8212\'20\'14including pro-lifers. So Mr. Trump released a list of 11 potential nominees from which he'd choose to fill the empty seat.\par \par \uc2 It was a bold move, and he deserves credit for that too. Equally bold was his decision to speak bluntly about partial-birth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his debate with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , highlighting Democratic extremism. He characterized her position as "in the ninth month, you can take the baby and rip the baby out of the womb of the mother just prior to the birth of the baby."\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Clinton tried to push back, but Mr. Trump made his point. On Sunday Ms. Welker tried to "fact check" him when he again talked about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to nine months, saying "Democrats aren't saying that." Of course they don't say that because voters would be repelled. But they fight any limit on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including partial-birth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ed Whelan\uc2  is president emeritus of the Ethics and Public Policy Center. "It's one thing for pro-lifers to be grateful to Trump for appointing Supreme Court justices who were critical in overturning Roe," he tweeted Sunday after the "Meet the Press" interview. "It's quite another to think we should view him as an ally going forward."\par \par \uc2 As for going forward, there are two realities about a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Former South Carolina Gov. Nikki Haley pointed to one in the first GOP debate last month: Republicans don't have the 60 Senate votes needed.\par \par \uc2 The other is that those hoping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will go away as a national issue are dreaming. So long as the Democrats are pushing to codify Roe in federal law, voters will demand to know where candidates for president and Congress stand on the issue\u8212\'20\'14and they will have to answer. Republicans will also have to respond when Democrats insist on constitutionally dubious policies, such as the Pentagon's decision to underwrite, without the blessing of Congress, interstate travel by service members to obtain abortions.\par \par \uc2 In short, Mr. Trump can rightfully claim credit for making Dobbs possible. But as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics enters the post-Dobbs future, let's not forget it was the pro-life movement that made \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  possible.\par \par \uc2 Write to mcgurn@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020230918ej9i005h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
